WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:03.850
In Sidney Prescott’s room, next to her desk,
there is a poster for Swamp Ophelia, an album

00:00:03.850 --> 00:00:07.944
by The Indigo Girls, a folk-rock band from the 1980s. 
As you can see on this poster,

00:00:07.944 --> 00:00:11.250
the band is a 2-piece. The latest single from
that album at the time Scream came out was

00:00:11.250 --> 00:00:13.030
also called “The Power of Two.”

00:00:13.030 --> 00:00:16.317
This is symbolic because of the twist in Scream:
the fact that there are two killers.

00:00:16.317 --> 00:00:20.519
The power of two is the reason that they were able to
trick police and clear Billy’s name as a suspect.

00:00:20.519 --> 00:00:23.930
With this in mind, we can go back
and find many more clues about Scream’s

00:00:23.930 --> 00:00:27.671
double trouble antagonists. To learn the connection
between Stu Macher’s room and the video

00:00:27.671 --> 00:00:30.359
rental store, stick around to the end of this
video.

00:00:30.359 --> 00:00:34.800
♪ Metal Music ♪

00:00:35.982 --> 00:00:39.860
Everybody knows that Scream is a satire, a
horror movie that makes a commentary about

00:00:39.860 --> 00:00:43.912
horror movies. But I think a lot of people
overlook what a satire actually is. So let’s

00:00:43.912 --> 00:00:46.113
take it back to the basic definition.

00:00:46.113 --> 00:00:51.180
Satire is the use of humor, irony, exaggeration,
or ridicule to expose and criticize something.

00:00:51.180 --> 00:00:55.917
By that definition, satire has always been
around, long before Scream and long before movies.

00:00:55.917 --> 00:00:59.109
Even the slasher genre had already
come out with films like Student Bodies or

00:00:59.109 --> 00:01:02.770
Evil Dead 2. So let's just drop the charade
that Scream is some kind of first of it's

00:01:02.770 --> 00:01:03.770
kind movie.

00:01:03.770 --> 00:01:07.290
What one of the things that makes Scream loved
by many is its ability to satirize without

00:01:07.290 --> 00:01:10.720
turning to comedy, which led to it being a
legitimate force in the genre that it was

00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:15.470
aiming to expose. In fact, Scream was so successful,
and so many tried to imitate it, that it’s

00:01:15.470 --> 00:01:17.540
acute self-awareness became the norm for a
while.

00:01:17.540 --> 00:01:21.390
Audiences had seen this happen in television
a few years earlier with The Simpsons, the

00:01:21.390 --> 00:01:24.710
family sitcom which attacked the positive
family values of other sitcoms on the air

00:01:24.710 --> 00:01:25.710
at the time.

00:01:25.710 --> 00:01:28.829
– [Lisa] Wow, it must be expensive to produce
all these cartoons.

00:01:28.829 --> 00:01:33.430
– [Producer] Well, we cut corners. Sometimes
to save money, our animators will reuse the

00:01:33.430 --> 00:01:36.079
same backgrounds over and over and over again.

00:01:36.079 --> 00:01:39.500
As a result, everybody wanted to make
a show with that cutting edge social commentary

00:01:39.500 --> 00:01:43.689
like The Simpsons. But the Simpsons was a
comedy; a comedy that made fun of the TV families

00:01:43.689 --> 00:01:48.510
of 80s, then surpassed them and became the
TV family of the 90s. Scream was able to follow

00:01:48.510 --> 00:01:52.409
this blueprint, but for horror movies. You
can make the argument that Scream is funny,

00:01:52.409 --> 00:01:55.560
but it’s a different kind of funny; it’s
pretty clear the intention is not to have

00:01:55.560 --> 00:02:00.210
you – as they put it in the 90s – ROFL.
That isn’t to say screenwriter Kevin Williamson

00:02:00.210 --> 00:02:04.189
and director Wes Craven don’t understand
the formula of setup and punchline. They actually

00:02:04.189 --> 00:02:08.030
transform that concept and use it for some
of the scares in the movie instead of laughs.

00:02:08.030 --> 00:02:11.640
For example, there are a number of scenes
that end with a big push-in to a spot in the

00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:14.990
background, where Ghostface can then be seen
lurking. We see this outside of Sid’s house,

00:02:14.990 --> 00:02:19.060
and at the grocery store, so we start to associate
that particular camera movement with the appearance

00:02:19.060 --> 00:02:22.980
of our antagonist. When Tatum is getting beers
from the garage, we see the camera brush past

00:02:22.980 --> 00:02:27.040
her and focus on the doorframe. It starts
to elevate our heart rates, but then, nothing

00:02:27.040 --> 00:02:30.920
happens. That’s such a clever way to build
suspense. Then we see the door close, but

00:02:30.920 --> 00:02:34.510
it’s just the cat, so we’re no longer
on edge, making it all the more effective

00:02:34.510 --> 00:02:37.500
when Ghostface does appear in front of the
closed door.

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:40.780
There are other techniques like this used
to throw off the audience, and of course some

00:02:40.780 --> 00:02:44.870
clever easter eggs from Wes Craven, so let’s
get into the Things You Missed.

00:02:44.870 --> 00:02:46.870
(SLICE)

00:02:49.758 --> 00:02:54.159
Even the title, Scream, feels like it’s
poking fun at some of the so-simple-it-could-be-about-anything

00:02:54.159 --> 00:03:00.360
titles of Alfred Hitchcock’s horror movies,
like Blackmail! Murder! Sabotage! Rope! And

00:03:00.360 --> 00:03:04.750
of course, Psycho! The beginning of Scream
pulls out a trick that was used in Psycho.

00:03:04.750 --> 00:03:09.019
Psycho came out in 1960 and it’s biggest
star was it’s female lead Janet Leigh, who

00:03:09.019 --> 00:03:13.360
played Marion Crane. She is prominently featured
on the poster, so audiences were totally shocked

00:03:13.360 --> 00:03:15.700
when they killed her off very early on in
the movie.

00:03:15.700 --> 00:03:19.170
Scream’s biggest star is Drew Barrymore,
who is similarly the face of the marketing

00:03:19.170 --> 00:03:22.950
campaign, so many people were shocked to see
her brutally disemboweled in the opening scene.

00:03:22.950 --> 00:03:26.910
There’s kind of a lineage that exists connecting
the most prominent slasher movie of each generation.

00:03:26.910 --> 00:03:30.110
When I covered Halloween here on Things You
Missed, I spent a lot of time talking about

00:03:30.110 --> 00:03:34.420
how it was inspired by Psycho. Now the torch
is being passed down again to Scream. We can

00:03:34.420 --> 00:03:39.459
see this in the name: Loomis. Psycho had Samuel
Loomis, Halloween had Dr. Sam Loomis, and

00:03:39.459 --> 00:03:43.549
Scream had Billy Loomis, who, after revealing
he is one of the killers, drops a quote from

00:03:43.549 --> 00:03:44.218
Psycho.

00:03:45.351 --> 00:03:47.951
–[Billy] We all go a little mad sometimes.

00:03:47.951 --> 00:03:51.663
– Billy is also most likely the one on the
phone who asks Casey to guess

00:03:51.663 --> 00:03:52.663
his favorite horror movie.

00:03:53.200 --> 00:03:57.129
– [Casey] A Nightmare on Elm Street!
– [Caller] I like that movie, it was scary.

00:03:57.129 --> 00:03:59.670
– [Casey] Well the first one was, but the
rest sucked.

00:03:59.670 --> 00:04:03.300
I was about to get up here and say that this
was a joke by director Wes Craven, who also

00:04:03.300 --> 00:04:06.870
directed the first Nightmare on Elm Street,
but not the five sequels that would follow.

00:04:06.870 --> 00:04:10.540
But apparently it was in the script before
it was ever decided that Craven would be the

00:04:10.540 --> 00:04:11.540
director.

00:04:11.540 --> 00:04:14.900
– [Craven] I kinda wanted to take that out,
I did not write that line.

00:04:14.900 --> 00:04:18.430
That answers my other question about that
line. Wes returned to the franchise to direct

00:04:18.430 --> 00:04:21.880
Wes Craven’s New Nightmare, in case you
didn’t know who the director of Wes Craven’s

00:04:21.880 --> 00:04:25.479
New Nightmare was… it was Wes Craven, which
was the most recent Freddy Krueger movie at

00:04:25.479 --> 00:04:29.290
the time Scream was made. It just seemed strange
that he would diss himself like that. Apparently

00:04:29.290 --> 00:04:32.979
the line later on, where Tatum mixes him up
with Halloween director John Carpenter…

00:04:32.979 --> 00:04:36.639
–[Tatum] Don't go there, Sid. You’re starting
to sound like some Wes Carpenter flick or

00:04:36.639 --> 00:04:37.639
something.

00:04:37.639 --> 00:04:39.770
…was also in the script before
Craven’s involvement, but it kind of works

00:04:39.770 --> 00:04:43.610
better now, seeing as how Scream uses Halloween
as a template for the horror movie tropes

00:04:43.610 --> 00:04:44.610
that it satirizes.

00:04:44.610 --> 00:04:48.950
After a few aggressive anonymous calls, Casey
immediately picks up the phone and yells…

00:04:48.950 --> 00:04:49.950
–[Casey] Listen ***hole!

00:04:49.950 --> 00:04:54.680
What if that had been someone else calling
just then? That would have been very rude

00:04:54.680 --> 00:04:55.680
Casey!

00:04:55.680 --> 00:04:58.419
After breaking into the house, we see Ghostface
looking around for her. My question is:

00:04:58.419 --> 00:05:01.530
why doesn’t he just try calling the phone again
and going towards the sound of the ringtone?

00:05:01.530 --> 00:05:04.940
It seems logical that she would keep the phone
on her to try to get help, so she might have

00:05:04.940 --> 00:05:06.190
forgotten to silence it.

00:05:06.190 --> 00:05:09.180
After Ghostface catches up with her, there
are a couple of easter eggs that play out

00:05:09.180 --> 00:05:11.630
on the Beckers’ side lawn.

00:05:11.630 --> 00:05:14.449
(THUNDER)

00:05:16.019 --> 00:05:20.100
♪ Industrial Music ♪

00:05:20.100 --> 00:05:23.840
As I mentioned, Halloween is the main template
that Scream uses for it’s satire. It’s

00:05:23.840 --> 00:05:26.720
the movie that Casey is planning to watch
in the opening, and it’s the movie that

00:05:26.720 --> 00:05:30.490
crushes Stu’s head in the finale. There’s
another Halloween connection after Casey manages

00:05:30.490 --> 00:05:34.150
to get outside and sees her parents going
back to the house. Despite the title of this

00:05:34.150 --> 00:05:37.509
movie, she is unable to find her scream and
get her parents attention.

00:05:37.509 --> 00:05:39.810
–[Casey] (barely audible) Mom…

00:05:39.810 --> 00:05:43.320
In Halloween II, we see this same phenomenon
take place as Laurie Strode tries to yell

00:05:43.320 --> 00:05:45.930
for help outside of Haddonfield Hospital.

00:05:45.930 --> 00:05:49.820
–[Laurie] (barely audible) Help… help...

00:05:49.820 --> 00:05:52.720
Then there’s yet another Halloween reference
just moments later.

00:05:52.720 --> 00:05:57.260
–[Mr. Becker] Get in the car. Drive down to the MacKenzies’.
Call the police.

00:05:57.260 --> 00:06:00.910
– When Laurie and the kids are targeted
by Michael Myers, she gives them a similar

00:06:00.910 --> 00:06:01.910
order.

00:06:01.910 --> 00:06:04.210
–[Laurie] I want you to go down the street to the
MacKenzies' house. I want you to tell them

00:06:04.210 --> 00:06:05.210
to call the police.

00:06:05.210 --> 00:06:08.669
I’m sure there will be a lot of people
that already know about that easter egg. And

00:06:08.669 --> 00:06:11.590
honestly, quite a few of the eggs in this
movie are pretty common knowledge because

00:06:11.590 --> 00:06:15.110
of the nature of Scream; it’s basically
a movie that’s so loaded with references

00:06:15.110 --> 00:06:18.050
and nods, that it’s going to attract people
who already know about that stuff.

00:06:18.050 --> 00:06:21.620
On Things You Missed™, I try to stick to
things you might have missed, that’s why

00:06:21.620 --> 00:06:24.940
it’s called that, if you didn’t know.
However, if I leave out the ones that everyone

00:06:24.940 --> 00:06:28.199
knows about then there are gonna be people
in the comments complaining that I missed stuff.

00:06:28.199 --> 00:06:31.289
So let’s just go through the ones
that I think are more obvious real quick and

00:06:31.289 --> 00:06:32.289
get them out of the way.

00:06:32.289 --> 00:06:35.770
Billy climbs through Sidney's window and startles
her, just like Glen did to Nancy in A Nightmare

00:06:35.770 --> 00:06:38.970
on Elm Street. Sheriff Burke is played by
the actor who plays the police officer in

00:06:38.970 --> 00:06:43.110
Nightmare on Elm St. Tatum’s number 10 crop
top is very similar to a shirt worn by Glen

00:06:43.110 --> 00:06:47.039
in A Nightmare on Elm St. Principal Himbry
has the following conversation with the janitor.

00:06:47.039 --> 00:06:51.199
– [Himbry] Damn little sh*ts
– [Janitor] What’d you call me?

00:06:51.199 --> 00:06:52.590
– [Himbry] Not you Fred.

00:06:52.590 --> 00:06:56.190
That’s director Wes Craven cameoing
as Fred, which is short for Freddy, because

00:06:56.190 --> 00:06:59.190
he’s dressed up like Freddy Krueger from
A Nightmare On Elm Street.

00:06:59.190 --> 00:07:00.400
– [Rizzo] Frederick! Freddie!

00:07:00.400 --> 00:07:04.199
When Ghostface pins down Sidney, he cocks
his head just like Michael Myers, and the

00:07:04.199 --> 00:07:07.639
movie makes a point to show that scene during
Stu’s party. Sidney refers to the town of

00:07:07.639 --> 00:07:11.650
Woodsboro under curfew as The Town That Dreaded
Sundown. And Randy, who yells at the TV to

00:07:11.650 --> 00:07:15.229
warn Jamie Lee Curtis about the threat behind
her, also has a threat behind him.

00:07:15.229 --> 00:07:19.099
– [Randy] Jamie, look behind you. Look behind
you.

00:07:19.099 --> 00:07:22.400
The actor that plays Randy is also named
Jamie, and he’s being watched in a similar

00:07:22.400 --> 00:07:24.460
manner by Kenny, the news camera guy.

00:07:24.460 --> 00:07:27.860
And speaking of people named Jamie, Sidney
at one point escapes from the attic out onto

00:07:27.860 --> 00:07:29.759
the roof like Jamie from Halloween 4.

00:07:29.759 --> 00:07:33.139
I think those are all the more well known
easter eggs, so let's get into the fun stuff:

00:07:33.139 --> 00:07:34.290
the less obvious ones.

00:07:34.290 --> 00:07:37.991
When Billy sneaks into Sid’s room, he tells
her he was watching the Exorcist on TV, with

00:07:37.991 --> 00:07:41.440
all the good parts cut out, and it made him
think of their relationship. From this point,

00:07:41.440 --> 00:07:44.900
they continue to reference back to this idea
that their relationship is like a movie. After

00:07:44.900 --> 00:07:47.229
Sid’s close call with Ghostface, a reporter
asks her…

00:07:47.229 --> 00:07:51.813
–[Reporter] So how does it feel to be almost brutally
butchered? People want to know, they have a right to know.

00:07:51.819 --> 00:07:57.090
And this is a cameo by Linda Blair, she
played Regan in The Exorcist 23 years earlier.

00:07:57.090 --> 00:07:59.820
If you’re listening closely during this
Sidney and Billy scene, you’ll notice that

00:07:59.820 --> 00:08:03.009
there is a song playing in the background.
It’s an acoustic cover of Don’t Fear The

00:08:03.009 --> 00:08:07.139
Reaper by Blue Oyster Cult. A song that I
like, but I am starting to get a little tired

00:08:07.139 --> 00:08:11.050
of hearing in movies and TV. But I’ll save
that rant for a future video, because here,

00:08:11.050 --> 00:08:14.260
there might be a good reason for it: it’s
the same song that Laurie Strode and Annie

00:08:14.260 --> 00:08:18.260
Brackett listen to in the car in Halloween.
And as I’ve said many times already, Halloween

00:08:18.260 --> 00:08:20.009
is the template that Scream builds off of.

00:08:20.009 --> 00:08:23.610
There could also be a second meaning here,
the Ghostface costume is essentially a depiction

00:08:23.610 --> 00:08:27.370
of a grim reaper. The name of the costume
itself in universe is “Father Death.”

00:08:27.370 --> 00:08:31.310
The next day the school is buzzing with rumors
and news crews trying to capitalize on the story.

00:08:31.310 --> 00:08:34.979
We first see one of our main characters,
Gail Weathers, in the background of an establishing shot.

00:08:34.979 --> 00:08:38.339
Even though we don’t end up going
towards her here, the neon green outfit in

00:08:38.339 --> 00:08:41.700
a sea of dull wardrobe colors draws the eye
and lets us know she’s important. It’s

00:08:41.700 --> 00:08:45.060
It's like in a video game where it will only let
you target characters that you can actually

00:08:45.060 --> 00:08:49.060
talk to. That’s good filmmaking. It’s
also kind of a preview of how the movie ends,

00:08:49.060 --> 00:08:51.340
but Gail has a bloody lip and a red dress.

00:08:51.340 --> 00:08:54.630
During school that day, each student is called
into the office for interrogation. There’s

00:08:54.630 --> 00:08:58.291
a chart on the wall that details which teachers
are in which classrooms each period. Most

00:08:58.291 --> 00:09:01.820
of the teachers’ names are crew reference
easter eggs. Almost all of these refer to

00:09:01.820 --> 00:09:05.500
people in the art department, who would have
been the ones to put these decorations on

00:09:05.500 --> 00:09:09.630
set. They include carpenter Art Williams,
local lead person Kenny Montante, additional

00:09:09.630 --> 00:09:14.120
set decorator Debra Echard, leadman Richard
Lambert, set dresser Josh Elliot, art department

00:09:14.120 --> 00:09:18.589
production assistant Charlene Bar, art director
David Lubin, driver Ben Davis, and set decorator

00:09:18.589 --> 00:09:22.329
Michele Poulik, probably a very unfortunate
name to have to grow up with.

00:09:22.329 --> 00:09:25.649
We actually see Michele put her name into
the movie a couple times. In the hallway,

00:09:25.649 --> 00:09:29.450
there’s a poster advertising Michelle Palik
for President, a deliberate misspelling of

00:09:29.450 --> 00:09:33.170
her name. In the bathroom, there’s some
graffiti that says “Michele + Bob 4 ever”,

00:09:33.170 --> 00:09:36.000
using the same unconventional spelling as
the set decorator.

00:09:36.000 --> 00:09:39.190
Because of her situation with her mom being
killed a year prior, the media attention is

00:09:39.190 --> 00:09:40.490
very familiar for Sid.

00:09:40.490 --> 00:09:44.260
– [Sidney] You know the police and reporters
and everything, it’s like Deja Vu all over again.

00:09:45.260 --> 00:09:47.850
That’s a redundant statement. That would
be like saying I went on a side-tangent. Or,

00:09:47.850 --> 00:09:51.080
if I commented on Scream’s acute self-awareness.

00:09:51.080 --> 00:09:55.200
One of the things Scream does to stay interesting
is play with our expectations. Pay attention

00:09:55.200 --> 00:09:59.480
to the music sting that happens when Sidney
opens the closet door.

00:09:59.480 --> 00:10:05.910
(ghostly whisper in music)

00:10:05.910 --> 00:10:09.410
Using typical horror movie logic, the audience
can expect that this means a scare is about

00:10:09.410 --> 00:10:12.891
to happen. It’s the perfect setup for that
classic refrigerator door scare, but when

00:10:12.891 --> 00:10:16.990
she closes it, nobody is there. This also
establishes the idea that the closet should

00:10:16.990 --> 00:10:19.794
be empty, making it even better when Ghostface
busts out of it 5 minutes later.

00:10:19.794 --> 00:10:21.920
– [Ghostface] With the murders and all,
it’s like

00:10:21.920 --> 00:10:24.980
right out of a horror movie or something.

00:10:24.980 --> 00:10:28.800
We see more of that aforementioned self-awareness
as Sidney mocks horror movies, saying they

00:10:28.800 --> 00:10:29.800
are all the same.

00:10:29.800 --> 00:10:33.230
–[Sidney]  …some stupid killer stalking some big
breasted girl who can’t act, who’s always

00:10:33.230 --> 00:10:36.110
running up the stairs when she should be going
out the front door, it’s insulting.

00:10:36.110 --> 00:10:39.300
…only for her to do just that when she
can’t figure out how to unlock the front door.

00:10:39.300 --> 00:10:43.170
I always love this next part. She calls
the police on her PC, connects to someone

00:10:43.170 --> 00:10:47.550
seconds later, and the cops are at the door
in 46 seconds. Take notes Uber Eats.

00:10:47.550 --> 00:10:51.700
So Sidney is saved by the time Tatum shows
up in her red VW Beetle. I don’t know 100%

00:10:51.700 --> 00:10:55.990
if this is an intentional reference, but since
it’s Scream, I’ll just assume that it is.

00:10:55.990 --> 00:10:59.899
This is the car that Jack Torrance drives
in the novel version of The Shining. It appears

00:10:59.899 --> 00:11:02.899
in Stanley Kubrick’s film adaptation as
well, as the car that’s been crushed by

00:11:02.899 --> 00:11:06.560
an overturned truck, Kubrick’s way of saying,
screw you King, I’m not gonna follow the book.

00:11:06.560 --> 00:11:10.339
Of course, there is a more obvious Stephen
King reference later on.

00:11:10.339 --> 00:11:13.850
–[Billy] Corn syrup, same stuff they use for pig’s
blood in Carrie.

00:11:13.850 --> 00:11:17.450
Tatum also goes with to the police station,
where they get some info from Sidney. 

00:11:17.450 --> 00:11:20.530
Her brother Dewey tries to get her to respect
him while he’s in uniform in front of his

00:11:20.530 --> 00:11:21.860
superior officer.

00:11:21.860 --> 00:11:25.050
– [Dewey] He’s my superior.
– [Tatum] The janitor’s your superior.

00:11:25.050 --> 00:11:30.420
Which is true… on the film set, since
Wes Craven, the director, plays the janitor.

00:11:30.420 --> 00:11:32.079
Technically he’s David Arquette’s boss.

00:11:32.079 --> 00:11:35.990
Billy’s name is cleared when Stu calls Sidney
again using the Ghostface voice while Billy

00:11:35.990 --> 00:11:39.550
is still in custody. Sheriff Burke is no L.
I don’t even know if he’s on the level

00:11:39.550 --> 00:11:40.889
of Randy, to be honest.

00:11:40.889 --> 00:11:42.970
–[Randy] He’s got killer printed all over his
forehead.

00:11:42.970 --> 00:11:45.870
Which is exactly where Sidney shoots him
at the end of the movie.

00:11:45.870 --> 00:11:49.480
Randy is portrayed as the biggest buff in
the story. He works at the video store, and

00:11:49.480 --> 00:11:53.139
knows all the rules about horror movies. We
see these rules referenced time and time again

00:11:53.139 --> 00:11:56.540
by the killer on the phone, and that leads
us to see Randy as a prime suspect.

00:11:56.540 --> 00:11:59.930
–[Randy] I’m the first to admit it. If this were
a scary movie, I’d be the prime suspect.

00:11:59.930 --> 00:12:03.870
However, there may be someone who is an
equally big movie buff, and that is Stu Macher.

00:12:03.870 --> 00:12:07.589
In fact, there are a couple clues that link
him to the video store just as much as Randy.

00:12:07.589 --> 00:12:12.019
The store has these two posters on display.
The one on the left is a poster for the 1993

00:12:12.019 --> 00:12:16.589
film Mother’s Boys, starring Jamie Lee Curtis.
Stu is a big fan of Jamie Lee. All the videos

00:12:16.589 --> 00:12:18.201
he rents for his party have her in them.

00:12:18.201 --> 00:12:23.531
–[Sidney] Terror train. Prom night. How come Jamie
Lee Curtis is in all of these movies?

00:12:23.531 --> 00:12:27.046
–[Randy] She’s the Scream Queen.

00:12:27.046 --> 00:12:29.339
– [Stu] And with a set of lungs like that, she should be.

00:12:29.339 --> 00:12:32.890
On the right is a poster for the band
Greenjelly. This same poster can be seen when

00:12:32.890 --> 00:12:37.290
Sidney runs into Stu’s room to escape Ghostface,
so I think it just serves as a link to show

00:12:37.290 --> 00:12:41.096
us that Stu probably spends more time than
we’d expect at the video store.

00:12:41.096 --> 00:12:43.864
At this point in the movie, there are a few innuendoes as well. 

00:12:43.864 --> 00:12:48.936
Tatum tells Sidney her intimacy issues will thaw out with time as she removes a case of popsicles from the freezer.

00:12:48.936 --> 00:12:51.959
This basically tells us that Sidney's
getting ready to lose her virginity.

00:12:51.959 --> 00:12:56.399
Then there’s this… meme-worthy moment.
Our eyes are drawn to a certain part of the

00:12:56.399 --> 00:13:00.459
frame, which of course is Tatum’s lungs.
Then our eyes are drawn to another part of

00:13:00.459 --> 00:13:04.500
the frame. This spiral pattern just kind of
draws you in, it’s like a hypnosis. Even

00:13:04.500 --> 00:13:08.279
though we don’t see her having sex, we know
that Tatum falls victim to rule number one,

00:13:08.279 --> 00:13:11.269
because her death occurs when she gets stuck
in the pet door in the garage, and she’s

00:13:11.269 --> 00:13:14.709
unable to get out because her tits and cheeks
are too big.

00:13:14.709 --> 00:13:18.500
Also the song playing when she enters the
garage is "Drop Dead Gorgeous" by Republica.

00:13:18.500 --> 00:13:21.899
Scream was doing death omen songs four years
before Final Destination came out.

00:13:21.899 --> 00:13:25.720
That night, Sidney does end up giving up the
v-card to Billy, and Ghostface breaks in and

00:13:25.720 --> 00:13:29.899
appears to kill him, but obviously we know
it was just corn syrup. The was Stu cleans

00:13:29.899 --> 00:13:33.579
up off the knife is interesting to me. The
motion almost kind of resembles taking off

00:13:33.579 --> 00:13:37.170
a condom, and for Sidney, it comes immediately
as a post-sex act.

00:13:37.170 --> 00:13:40.540
And Stu later admits that he did have a thing
for Sidney, which she responds to by pushing

00:13:40.540 --> 00:13:41.850
the TV onto him and saying

00:13:41.850 --> 00:13:43.000
–[Sidney] In your dreams.

00:13:43.000 --> 00:13:45.300
(Stu SCREAMing; source of movie title)

00:13:45.300 --> 00:13:48.750
It’s kind of a karma moment, because
Stu was the one to laugh in Randy’s face

00:13:48.750 --> 00:13:50.610
when he asked if he might have a chance with
Sidney.

00:13:50.610 --> 00:13:53.970
After everything we’ve been through, it’s
only fitting that the action ends with one

00:13:53.970 --> 00:13:55.233
last meta-horror trope.

00:13:56.721 --> 00:14:01.351
– [Randy] This is the moment when the supposedly
dead killer comes back to life. For one last scare.

00:14:01.351 --> 00:14:02.550
–[Billy] Augh!

00:14:02.550 --> 00:14:04.269
(screams)
(gunshot)

00:14:04.269 --> 00:14:05.269
–[Sidney] Not in my movie.

00:14:05.269 --> 00:14:07.670
What do you mean not in my movie? It literally
just happened.

00:14:07.670 --> 00:14:11.170
Before we can end this video, there is one
more thing I want to talk about.

00:14:11.170 --> 00:14:14.545
(THUNDER)

00:14:16.034 --> 00:14:19.390
♪ Upbeat Music ♪

00:14:19.390 --> 00:14:23.709
The big surprise in Scream, that again, like
the camerawork and writing choices that I

00:14:23.709 --> 00:14:27.560
brought up in this video, uses our horror-genre
expectations against us, is the fact that

00:14:27.560 --> 00:14:31.080
there are two killers instead of the usual
one that we get in most slasher movies.

00:14:31.080 --> 00:14:34.801
The clues were there all along. In the Casey
Becker scene, one Ghostface breaks into the

00:14:34.801 --> 00:14:38.140
house and runs straight to the front door.
We don’t know why at the time, but it was

00:14:38.140 --> 00:14:42.079
to let his accomplice inside. You can actually
spot that the door is now open when Casey’s

00:14:42.079 --> 00:14:43.139
parents get home.

00:14:43.139 --> 00:14:46.949
At the fountain, Stu explains to Sidney how
you gut someone, Billy makes eye contact with

00:14:46.949 --> 00:14:47.970
him and says,

00:14:47.970 --> 00:14:50.530
–[Billy] Hey, it’s called tact you f*ckrag.

00:14:50.530 --> 00:14:53.770
We assume that he’s standing up for
his girlfriend, but he might actually be trying

00:14:53.770 --> 00:14:56.410
to shut Stu up before he says too much and
gives them away.

00:14:56.410 --> 00:15:00.491
This might be obvious, but Sidney first suspects
Billy when she sees the cell phone fall out

00:15:00.491 --> 00:15:03.550
of his pocket. If he has a cell phone, why
does he need to come sneak through the window

00:15:03.550 --> 00:15:04.550
to talk to her?

00:15:04.550 --> 00:15:08.760
Then there are multiple instances of Billy
just acting sus. Like when Sidney admits that

00:15:08.760 --> 00:15:11.709
she doesn’t think he’s guilty, he keeps
trying to convince her.

00:15:11.709 --> 00:15:19.089
–[Billy] You still think it’s me?
–[Sidney] No I don’t, it’s just somebody was there, Billy. Someone tried to kill me.

00:15:19.089 --> 00:15:23.000
–[Billy] I know, I know, the cops said I scared him away. It wasn’t me Sid.

00:15:23.000 --> 00:15:26.351
–[Sidney] I know. He called me again last night at Tatum’s house.

00:15:26.351 --> 00:15:29.209
–[Billy] See? Couldn’t have been me, I was in jail.

00:15:29.209 --> 00:15:32.569
And this is obviously him trying too hard
to drive home the point that the second Ghostface

00:15:32.569 --> 00:15:33.720
call exonerates him.

00:15:33.720 --> 00:15:35.100
Stu does a similar thing at the fountain.

00:15:35.100 --> 00:15:38.740
– [Stu] No, I didn’t kill anybody.
– [Billy] Nobody said you did.

00:15:38.740 --> 00:15:41.830
Then at the school, there are a
couple of pranksters who run around in Ghostface

00:15:41.830 --> 00:15:45.230
masks, and they find themselves sitting in
the principal’s office. One is wearing blue

00:15:45.230 --> 00:15:48.709
flannel with a white t-shirt underneath, which
is what Billy wears to the party, and the

00:15:48.709 --> 00:15:52.529
other wearing a muted long sleeve shirt, not
the same color, but very similar in style

00:15:52.529 --> 00:15:54.040
to what Stu has on at the party.

00:15:54.040 --> 00:15:57.200
Now I want you to look very closely, right
here…

00:15:58.434 --> 00:15:59.434
(jumpscare)

00:15:59.434 --> 00:16:00.957
Had to get that one last scare in. 

00:16:00.957 --> 00:16:04.362
(THUNDER)

00:16:05.519 --> 00:16:07.519
(THUNDER)

00:16:09.717 --> 00:16:13.120
Throughout Scream 2, we see a few glimpses
of the movie Stab! which is essentially the

00:16:13.120 --> 00:16:17.389
in-universe version of Scream 1. Each character’s
Stab! counterpart is played by a known actor

00:16:17.389 --> 00:16:21.250
from our world, and in the case of Sidney,
that actress is Tori Spelling, which is playing

00:16:21.250 --> 00:16:22.839
off of a joke from the original Scream.

00:16:22.839 --> 00:16:26.699
– [Tatum] Hey, Sid, just think. If they
make a movie about you, who's gonna play you?

00:16:26.699 --> 00:16:29.205
–[Dewey] I see you as a young Meg Ryan myself.

00:16:29.205 --> 00:16:31.569
–[Sidney] Thanks Dewey, with my luck, they'd cast Tori Spelling.

00:16:31.569 --> 00:16:34.530
It’s at one point suggested that the
new killer is trying to make an in real life

00:16:34.530 --> 00:16:38.180
sequel to the happenings in Woodsboro from
the year before, and Scream 2 consequently

00:16:38.180 --> 00:16:42.009
features many scenes that call back to the
original. To learn the meaning behind the

00:16:42.009 --> 00:16:43.889
graffiti, stick around to the end of this
video.

00:16:43.889 --> 00:16:46.190
–[Joel] And I’ll be back when you guys start
talking about something a little more Saved

00:16:46.190 --> 00:16:47.190
by the Bell ish.

00:16:48.190 --> 00:16:52.830
Welcome to Things You Missed. I am Zac
Morris. As I discussed last episode, Scream 1

00:16:52.830 --> 00:16:57.699
was a satire on horror movies, so naturally
Scream 2 satirizes sequels. In fact, it openly

00:16:57.699 --> 00:17:01.769
acknowledges that they’re usually inferior
to the original… so what’s the deal here?

00:17:01.769 --> 00:17:05.820
Did they intentionally make Scream 2 an inferior
movie? In the movie premiere scene, Maureen

00:17:05.820 --> 00:17:10.110
gets flack from her fellow movie goers when
she *loudly* encourages the character on screen

00:17:10.110 --> 00:17:11.910
to use some common sense.

00:17:11.910 --> 00:17:16.569
Scream 2 features previously intelligent characters
doing things that make you want to *ahem*

00:17:16.569 --> 00:17:20.640
Scream at the television, like splitting up
with a killer in the vicinity, backing up

00:17:20.640 --> 00:17:24.690
against a window and going back to unmask
someone when you should just run.

00:17:24.690 --> 00:17:28.490
– [Randy] The way I see it, someone’s
out to make a sequel. You know, cash in on

00:17:28.490 --> 00:17:29.710
all the movie murder hoopla.

00:17:29.710 --> 00:17:35.490
…says Randy, as he enjoys his
product placement. Let’s get into the Things You Missed.

00:17:35.490 --> 00:17:39.620
♪ Mysterious Music ♪

00:17:39.620 --> 00:17:42.820
In the first Scream, I talked about how the
movie started by pulling a page from Alfred

00:17:42.820 --> 00:17:46.840
Hitchcock’s Psycho by killing off it's poster
girl. This time, the poster features two more

00:17:46.840 --> 00:17:51.010
big stars, Jada Pinkett and Sarah Michelle
Gellar. Jada’s character Maureen is the

00:17:51.010 --> 00:17:55.429
second to die after her boyfriend, just like
Casey, and Sarah’s character Cici is third.

00:17:55.429 --> 00:17:58.919
Also, speaking of pulling a page from Psycho,
the opening scene of Stab adds a part where

00:17:58.919 --> 00:18:02.870
Casey goes into the shower. The shot of the
tap turning on is the same shot from Psycho.

00:18:02.870 --> 00:18:06.980
The rest of the scene is pretty accurate to
what we saw in Scream, including the conversation,

00:18:06.980 --> 00:18:09.640
which begs the question: how would the creators
of Stab

00:18:09.640 --> 00:18:14.770
know what transpired that night unless…
one of the participants was actually still

00:18:14.770 --> 00:18:15.770
alive?

00:18:15.770 --> 00:18:19.640
Scream 1 used Halloween as a template, a movie
that everybody knows and can understand how

00:18:19.640 --> 00:18:23.950
it’s being satirized. But Scream 2 doesn’t
really offer an equivalent. However, Stab

00:18:23.950 --> 00:18:27.840
acts as an in-universe stand-in for Scream
1, and we’ve seen something similar in the

00:18:27.840 --> 00:18:31.740
Halloween franchise. In Halloween III, we
see that the original Halloween is just a

00:18:31.740 --> 00:18:35.910
movie in that universe. Halloween III is also
a famously panned sequel, but other than that,

00:18:35.910 --> 00:18:37.520
there aren’t really any other similarities.

00:18:37.520 --> 00:18:41.471
Scream had become famous and there were already
movies in production trying to imitate it.

00:18:41.471 --> 00:18:45.320
So the challenge was on for Scream 2 to try
to be the best imitator. While the first Scream

00:18:45.320 --> 00:18:49.220
made its gimmick apparent by having the killers
quiz Casey on scary movies while being in

00:18:49.220 --> 00:18:53.539
a scary movie, Scream 2 tells us that these
movie fans are about to be in a movie of their

00:18:53.539 --> 00:18:57.850
own by using screen direction. ‘Stab Casey’
is seen running to the left towards a door,

00:18:57.850 --> 00:19:01.240
and then we cut to Phil going left through
a door himself. This link is the director’s

00:19:01.240 --> 00:19:03.640
way of telling us that he’s about to be
a victim too.

00:19:03.640 --> 00:19:07.620
We hear the song Red Right Hand by Nick Cave
and the Bad Seeds twice in this movie. In

00:19:07.620 --> 00:19:11.080
the first one it played during the curfew
lockup montage, and in 2, we hear it right

00:19:11.080 --> 00:19:15.490
as Stab is beginning and as the Delta Lambda
Zeta party disperses, perhaps another link

00:19:15.490 --> 00:19:17.620
to tell us that the movie is leaking into
reality.

00:19:17.620 --> 00:19:22.270
There are a lot of instances where things
in Scream 2 fill the role for material from

00:19:22.270 --> 00:19:26.600
the first one. For example, Sidney’s college
mascot is the Wildcats, a less specific version

00:19:26.600 --> 00:19:29.941
of the Panthers, which was her high school
mascot. It plays into the idea that we have

00:19:29.941 --> 00:19:33.809
copycat killers, just like how the initial
3 victims share a name with the first three

00:19:33.809 --> 00:19:37.370
victims in Woodsboro. Sidney once again gets
unwanted attention from the press because

00:19:37.370 --> 00:19:40.780
of her past. The scene where Cici is attacked
at her sorority home is just like the scene

00:19:40.780 --> 00:19:45.110
of Sidney being attacked at her actual home.
And just like Scream 1, Ghostface pops out

00:19:45.110 --> 00:19:48.630
from a closet by the stairs. I wonder if the
fact that he hides “under the stairs”

00:19:48.630 --> 00:19:52.650
is a Wes Craven reference. Cici’s immediate
reaction is to then run up the stairs, just

00:19:52.650 --> 00:19:54.560
like high school Sidney said she would.

00:19:54.560 --> 00:19:58.030
–[Sidney] Some stupid killer stalking some big breasted
girl who can’t act who’s always running

00:19:58.030 --> 00:19:59.890
up the stairs when she should be going out
the front door.

00:19:59.890 --> 00:20:03.630
The ice cream scene is the new video store
scene. In the video store scene, Randy directly

00:20:03.630 --> 00:20:07.539
determines who one of the killers is by accusing
Billy, who fires back by asking…

00:20:07.539 --> 00:20:09.210
–[Billy] How do we know you’re not the killer?

00:20:09.210 --> 00:20:13.120
And Randy admits that if this were a scary
movie, he would, in fact, be a prime suspect.

00:20:13.120 --> 00:20:15.929
This time, he correctly identifies Mickey
as one of the killers.

00:20:15.929 --> 00:20:18.150
–[Randy] The freaky Tarantino film student.

00:20:18.150 --> 00:20:21.440
But also concedes that he would likely
be a suspect on the same grounds.

00:20:21.440 --> 00:20:23.470
– [Randy] But if he’s a suspect, so am I.

00:20:23.470 --> 00:20:26.419
Worth noting in this scene, he’s also
kind of on the right track when it comes to

00:20:26.419 --> 00:20:30.340
the second killer. He brings up Mrs. Voorhees
as proof that the killer could be a female.

00:20:30.340 --> 00:20:34.549
Like Mrs. Voorhees from Friday the 13th, the
killer is the mom of another killer. 

00:20:34.549 --> 00:20:35.840
Randy’s other suspect is Gale Weathers.

00:20:35.840 --> 00:20:38.419
–[Randy] That’s what reporters do, Dewey. They
stage the news.

00:20:38.419 --> 00:20:42.200
He’s got the right idea, but he’s
got the wrong reporter. History repeats itself

00:20:42.200 --> 00:20:45.610
later in the movie when Gale and Dewey team
up, and Gale makes an important discovery

00:20:45.610 --> 00:20:49.220
while they're hooking up. In the original
it’s Neil Prescott’s car, in the sequel

00:20:49.220 --> 00:20:52.210
it’s Mickey’s tapes. The killer is once
again behind Randy.

00:20:52.210 --> 00:20:53.500
–[Randy] Look behind you.

00:20:53.500 --> 00:20:56.500
Dewey gets stabbed in the back again.
Gale blocks the door to the recording studio

00:20:56.500 --> 00:21:00.660
so that Ghostface can only reach in and flail.
It’s like Sidney in her room. Mickey reveals

00:21:00.660 --> 00:21:03.280
himself as the killer using a familiar line.

00:21:03.280 --> 00:21:05.059
–[Mickey] Surprise Sidney!

00:21:05.059 --> 00:21:09.310
– …just like Stu did during the last 20
minutes of Scream.

00:21:09.310 --> 00:21:10.460
–[Stu] Surprise Sidney!

00:21:10.460 --> 00:21:14.039
– At the end, Sidney buries one into Mrs.
Loomis’s forehead just like she had to her

00:21:14.039 --> 00:21:16.970
son, and it all ends with Cotton foreshadowing
the next Stab film.

00:21:16.970 --> 00:21:20.519
– [Cotton] I’ll tell you one thing. It
will make a hell of a movie.

00:21:20.519 --> 00:21:24.919
– But there is one easter egg that even
I wouldn’t have found if not for this retrospective

00:21:24.919 --> 00:21:26.630
documentary.

00:21:26.630 --> 00:21:30.049
(THUNDER)

00:21:30.049 --> 00:21:33.470
♪ Dreadful Music ♪

00:21:33.470 --> 00:21:38.930
I’m sure you noticed the way Ghostface wipes
off the knife in Scream 2. It’s a signature

00:21:38.930 --> 00:21:42.299
move established by Stu in the first movie.
I wanted to bring it up though, because in

00:21:42.299 --> 00:21:46.970
my video on the first film, I tried to, as
I do, figure out what the subtext might be

00:21:46.970 --> 00:21:51.549
behind this. However, in the 2011 retrospective
Still Screaming, Dane Farwell, the stunt actor

00:21:51.549 --> 00:21:53.880
in that scene offers a simpler explanation.

00:21:53.880 --> 00:21:58.720
–[Dane] It’s a direct cut to me running through
the hall with a clean knife. Wes just thought

00:21:58.720 --> 00:22:00.920
for two seconds and said, “We’ll wipe
the knife off on camera.”

00:22:00.920 --> 00:22:04.580
– Another thing I talked about in my first
video was relevant cameo appearances, and

00:22:04.580 --> 00:22:07.940
those seem to have been given special attention
here in 1997.

00:22:07.940 --> 00:22:11.230
Director of Photography Peter Deming plays
the ‘Popcorn Boy’. Screenwriter Kevin

00:22:11.230 --> 00:22:14.539
Williamson is the interviewer on “Current
Edition”. I never would have noticed this

00:22:14.539 --> 00:22:19.049
on my own, but Matthew Lillard, who plays
Stu in Scream, used 2% of his power to make

00:22:19.049 --> 00:22:23.810
a background cameo at the party scene, which
thankfully for me, he brought up in the retrospective,

00:22:23.810 --> 00:22:25.660
otherwise I never would have known it was
him.

00:22:25.660 --> 00:22:29.430
–[Matthew] One day I was just hanging out and they
were like ‘come walk in the back of the

00:22:29.430 --> 00:22:30.430
party scene’.

00:22:30.430 --> 00:22:33.240
– And director Wes Craven makes another
appearance, this time as a doctor at the hospital

00:22:33.240 --> 00:22:36.630
scene. That one I actually found out about
from a fan site, which is coincidentally also

00:22:36.630 --> 00:22:40.850
called “Still Screaming.” The best cameo
though is this one, from Lev Shrieber’s dog.

00:22:40.850 --> 00:22:45.250
What a boy. There are a bunch of other
cameos, but on Things You Missed, I’m only

00:22:45.250 --> 00:22:48.930
interested in ones that have some kind of
significance or something to do with the movie.

00:22:48.930 --> 00:22:53.039
After a film class discussion on sequels and
imitators of movie killers, Gale brushes off

00:22:53.039 --> 00:22:55.050
the idea of audience safety being important.

00:22:55.050 --> 00:22:59.900
–[Gale] Are you kidding me? It’d be stupid to
pull this movie. All this free press they’re

00:22:59.900 --> 00:23:01.720
gonna have huge numbers this weekend.

00:23:01.720 --> 00:23:06.409
You either die a hero, or live long
enough to see yourself become the villain.

00:23:06.409 --> 00:23:10.270
Despite this movie’s concept: being a take-off
of horror sequels, we aren’t bombarded with

00:23:10.270 --> 00:23:14.230
movie references like we were in Scream. Instead,
there’s another theme that the finale centers

00:23:14.230 --> 00:23:18.300
itself around: theatre. Sidney is a theatre
major and has a prominent role in the production

00:23:18.300 --> 00:23:21.110
of Agamemnon, which is advertised all over
the campus.

00:23:21.110 --> 00:23:26.000
– [Gus] Cassandra is one of the great tragic
visionaries of literature. She saw it all

00:23:26.000 --> 00:23:32.010
coming. The wars, the murder, the madness.
She knew she was cursed. It was her fate and

00:23:32.010 --> 00:23:33.010
she embraced it.

00:23:33.010 --> 00:23:36.601
– This might be a comparison between two
unlikely subjects to be paired, horror movie

00:23:36.601 --> 00:23:40.620
final girls and greek mythology. One of the
things that audiences tend to find ridiculous

00:23:40.620 --> 00:23:44.670
about sequels is the fact that the same characters
get themselves into trouble again. It always

00:23:44.670 --> 00:23:48.720
seems like a huge coincidence that only exists
to extend the franchise. That’s why in a

00:23:48.720 --> 00:23:52.110
lot of long running franchises, there’s
some kind of curse that keeps the killer coming

00:23:52.110 --> 00:23:53.400
back again and again.

00:23:53.400 --> 00:23:56.990
The character Sidney plays in Agamemnon is
Cassandra, who is literally cursed by the

00:23:56.990 --> 00:24:01.059
Greek God Apollo. In the case of Scream, it’s
obviously not like a magic curse, but rather

00:24:01.059 --> 00:24:04.160
that Sidney is cursed by the publicity of
the horrible things that have happened to

00:24:04.160 --> 00:24:05.160
her.

00:24:05.160 --> 00:24:08.100
I do think it’s significant that their whole
culture on campus revolves around these sororities,

00:24:08.100 --> 00:24:11.940
or as they are sometimes called, Greek Life.
In the car crash scene, there’s some graffiti

00:24:11.940 --> 00:24:15.140
of the Greek letters representing the sorority
where the party took place earlier, Delta

00:24:15.140 --> 00:24:19.080
Lambda Zeta, along with text in English that
says “stab this”. But the Greek letters

00:24:19.080 --> 00:24:23.870
are crossed out in red, and this is the scene
where their pledge, Hallie, gets herself crossed

00:24:23.870 --> 00:24:24.870
off by Mickey.

00:24:24.870 --> 00:24:28.059
The movie’s climax takes place on the stage
where the “Fall of Troy” set is still

00:24:28.059 --> 00:24:31.870
up. This is where Ghostface attacks her on
more than one occasion. The Greek vs. the

00:24:31.870 --> 00:24:35.919
Trojan so to speak. At the end she actually
uses her Greek letters, given to her by her

00:24:35.919 --> 00:24:39.240
boyfriend to protect her, to attack Mickey,
and ultimately get away.

00:24:39.240 --> 00:24:43.960
The play that she’s in is Agamemnon, which
like Stab, is the first chapter of a trilogy.

00:24:43.960 --> 00:24:45.390
Scream was a trilogy from 2000-2011.

00:24:45.390 --> 00:24:46.390
(rock music)

00:24:46.390 --> 00:24:48.299
– [Mickey] Empire Strikes Back. It was a
better story. Improved effects.

00:24:48.299 --> 00:24:54.180
– [Randy] Not a sequel, part of a trilogy,
completely planned.

00:24:54.180 --> 00:24:57.690
– In Scream, I think I pointed out like
20 different Nightmare on Elm Street references,

00:24:57.690 --> 00:25:01.510
but Scream 2 seems to just have one, when
Cotton is arrested by Sidney’s bodyguards

00:25:01.510 --> 00:25:05.549
in the library we see a book called Our Dreaming
Mind, which deals with the science of sleep

00:25:05.549 --> 00:25:09.270
and dreams, much like how Nancy visits a sleep
disorder clinic to try fix her Freddy Fr--

00:25:09.270 --> 00:25:10.270
(BEEP)

00:25:10.270 --> 00:25:12.440
(laughing) to try to fix her Freddy Freeman
problem...

00:25:12.440 --> 00:25:13.440
(BEEP)

00:25:13.440 --> 00:25:14.520
...to try to fix her Freddy Krueger problem.

00:25:14.520 --> 00:25:18.360
For a minute, I thought this was Freddy’s
iconic sweater in Sid’s dorm room, but when

00:25:18.360 --> 00:25:21.710
I actually compared them, I realized they’re
not the same. I had the same thing happen

00:25:21.710 --> 00:25:25.179
in Sid’s room when I analyzed the first
Scream, I thought this was Chucky’s sweater,

00:25:25.179 --> 00:25:28.620
but eventually realized the colors don’t
match. Scream 2 has a girl that looks kind

00:25:28.620 --> 00:25:31.140
of like Chucky, but again, the colors don’t
match.

00:25:31.140 --> 00:25:34.289
As I did for the first movie, I want to wrap
up by talking about some of the evidence about

00:25:34.289 --> 00:25:38.470
who the killers are, some of which you might
have missed.

00:25:38.470 --> 00:25:41.669
(THUNDER)

00:25:41.669 --> 00:25:46.470
♪ Upbeat Music ♪

00:25:46.470 --> 00:25:50.061
First up we’ve got Cici’s sorority house,
where we can confirm that there are once again,

00:25:50.061 --> 00:25:53.809
two killers because you can still hear one
Ghostface talking on the phone as the other

00:25:53.809 --> 00:25:57.539
sneaks through the front door in the background.
During certain chase scenes, we hear Ghostface

00:25:57.539 --> 00:25:59.460
grunting. Sometimes it sounds male…

00:25:59.460 --> 00:26:01.269
(male grunting sounds)

00:26:01.269 --> 00:26:03.010
– … and at other times, the sound is female.

00:26:03.010 --> 00:26:04.260
(female grunting sounds)

00:26:04.260 --> 00:26:07.600
– But when speaking on the phone, we can’t
make this determination because of the voice

00:26:07.600 --> 00:26:12.250
changer. When Sidney gets a Ghostface call
from Debra Salt aka Mrs. Loomis at the sorority

00:26:12.250 --> 00:26:14.240
house, she hears this…

00:26:14.240 --> 00:26:17.980
– [Caller] Hello Sidney. Remember me?

00:26:17.980 --> 00:26:22.169
– …suggesting that it’s someone from
Sidney’s past, like her late ex-boyfriend’s

00:26:22.169 --> 00:26:23.169
mom.

00:26:23.169 --> 00:26:25.330
And from a filmmaking standpoint, when you
make a sequel, you have to include enough

00:26:25.330 --> 00:26:28.910
information so that even audiences who haven’t
seen the first one can keep up. We only see

00:26:28.910 --> 00:26:33.030
two scenes from Stab, the opening and this
scene, which reminds us that Billy’s Mom

00:26:33.030 --> 00:26:36.860
left him and his Dad. They only reason to
cut away from the scene we’re in and show

00:26:36.860 --> 00:26:40.580
that part is if that information is going
to somehow be important. Like if Billy’s

00:26:40.580 --> 00:26:43.059
Mom were to, for example, come back.

00:26:43.059 --> 00:26:46.679
Debra also suspiciously manages to get to
the scene of the crime at the sorority house

00:26:46.679 --> 00:26:50.850
where Cici was attacked and at the film school
where Dewey was attacked before anyone else.

00:26:50.850 --> 00:26:52.690
Maybe because she was already there.

00:26:52.690 --> 00:26:56.429
But the strongest evidence is actually the
evidence for Mickey being guilty. In class,

00:26:56.429 --> 00:26:59.409
he argues for the idea that sequels can be
better than the original. Of course he would

00:26:59.409 --> 00:27:02.029
say that. The Windsor murders are his sequel
to the Woodsboro murders.

00:27:02.029 --> 00:27:05.559
– [Sidney] You know, I knew this was coming?
I knew this wasn’t over?

00:27:05.559 --> 00:27:08.600
– [Mickey] You are not alone Sid.

00:27:08.600 --> 00:27:12.050
– Yeah, he would know that this was coming
because he was responsible. The most damning

00:27:12.050 --> 00:27:15.470
evidence though is the fact that he always
has his video camera. When Dewey and Gale

00:27:15.470 --> 00:27:19.330
go to study their tapes, they see each of
the crime scenes was recorded from Ghostface’s

00:27:19.330 --> 00:27:22.919
point of view. The only issue is that he isn’t
the one projecting these tapes. He must have

00:27:22.919 --> 00:27:26.520
given them to Mrs. Loomis at some point, because
he was planning on getting caught so he could

00:27:26.520 --> 00:27:28.820
use the trial to try to blame the movies for
his actions.

00:27:28.820 --> 00:27:32.470
OK, I take back what I said. The most damning
evidence against Mickey is the fact that we

00:27:32.470 --> 00:27:34.960
can see him right here through the eyeholes
of the Ghostface mask.

00:27:34.960 --> 00:27:39.070
Scream 2 was famously rewritten during production
because the ending leaked on the internet,

00:27:39.070 --> 00:27:42.590
so the fact that we have all these setups
is kind of impressive. We’re mostly all

00:27:42.590 --> 00:27:46.730
in agreement that Scream 1 is superior, and
I think the very idea that the sequel’s

00:27:46.730 --> 00:27:50.870
villain could just be switched like that is
kind of a testament to it being a weaker story.

00:27:50.870 --> 00:27:54.299
However, with all of that being said, I guess
Cotton was going to be one of the villains originally? 

00:27:54.299 --> 00:27:59.522
And I’m sort of glad that didn’t happen because his redemption at the end is one of the best parts.

00:27:59.522 --> 00:28:01.522
And it sets for Scream 3.

00:28:01.522 --> 00:28:05.055
If you’re a Kevin Smith fan, you may already
be familiar with Jay and Silent Bob’s cameo

00:28:05.055 --> 00:28:09.260
in Scream 3, but you might not know the backstory
behind it. Jay and Silent Bob are a pair of

00:28:09.260 --> 00:28:12.720
characters that first appeared in Kevin Smith’s
1994 movie, Clerks, which is prominently displayed

00:28:12.720 --> 00:28:16.240
at the video store in the original Scream.
Jay and Bob would become recurring characters

00:28:16.240 --> 00:28:19.950
who show up in many Kevin Smith films that
are part of his View Askewniverse, and as

00:28:19.950 --> 00:28:23.780
a thank you for the free advertising in Scream,
Kevin Smith and Scream director Wes Craven

00:28:23.780 --> 00:28:27.789
decided to exchange cameos in each other’s
movies. So, Jay and Silent Bob show up in

00:28:27.789 --> 00:28:31.970
Scream 3, and the following year, Wes shows
up in Jay and Silent Bob Strike Back, where

00:28:31.970 --> 00:28:36.220
he plays Wes Craven the director of Scream
4, a decade before Wes would actually create

00:28:36.220 --> 00:28:37.220
Scream 4.

00:28:37.220 --> 00:28:40.539
In addition to that, Cotton Weary’s apartment
in the opening of Scream 3 contains a copy

00:28:40.539 --> 00:28:43.669
of Chasing Amy, which is also part of the
View Askewniverse.

00:28:43.669 --> 00:28:47.309
The scene with Jay and Silent Bob also contains
a couple other cameos, which are pretty much

00:28:47.309 --> 00:28:51.020
only findable thanks to the retrospective
documentary Still Screaming, which points

00:28:51.020 --> 00:28:55.211
out a cameo from Wes Craven as the man with
a video camera, and Scream 3’s casting director

00:28:55.211 --> 00:28:56.710
Lisa Beach as the tour guide.

00:28:56.710 --> 00:29:01.101
To learn how Scream 3 may have had a second
killer who died before being revealed, stick

00:29:01.101 --> 00:29:05.850
around to the end of this video.

00:29:05.850 --> 00:29:12.110
(Thunder)

00:29:12.110 --> 00:29:16.860
In Scream, movies don’t create psychos,
movies make psychos more creative. In Scream 2

00:29:16.860 --> 00:29:21.110
movies are a scapegoat for someone who
is planning to be a killer. Scream 3 reverses

00:29:21.110 --> 00:29:25.299
this and has the killer make the movie, creating
his own lore every step of the way.

00:29:25.299 --> 00:29:29.100
Welcome to Things You Missed. Wanting to capitalize
on the hype train created by Scream 1 and 2

00:29:29.100 --> 00:29:33.419
producers decided to go ahead without screenwriter
Kevin Williamson, by replacing him with Ehren Kruger.

00:29:33.419 --> 00:29:37.700
Really? The creator of A Nightmare
On Elm Street is working with a guy named Kruger?

00:29:37.700 --> 00:29:38.700
– [Jennifer] Stage name.

00:29:38.700 --> 00:29:40.610
–[Bianca] You should talk, Judy Jurgenstern.

00:29:40.610 --> 00:29:41.610
– [Group] OHHHHH!!!!!

00:29:41.610 --> 00:29:45.360
– Sidney Prescott has become a crisis hotline
counselor, and she also takes on a stage name

00:29:45.360 --> 00:29:49.200
of sorts, going by Laura to her callers. She’s
unknowingly following in the footsteps of

00:29:49.200 --> 00:29:52.580
her mother, who used the name Rina Reynolds
while working in Hollywood. There’s a running

00:29:52.580 --> 00:29:56.820
theme of pseudonyms in Scream 3. Most of our
main characters are actors who are just as

00:29:56.820 --> 00:30:00.180
likely to respond to their character names
as their actual names, to the point that it

00:30:00.180 --> 00:30:02.100
becomes kind of a joke.

00:30:02.100 --> 00:30:06.520
–[Jennifer] Not “Sidney”, Sidney. Like I’m “Gale”
and she’s Gale.

00:30:06.520 --> 00:30:09.590
– Obviously, it’s more than just a joke
though, it’s important to the plot, because

00:30:09.590 --> 00:30:13.750
Roman’s changing of his last name to Bridger
is what helps disguise his true identity as

00:30:13.750 --> 00:30:15.169
Sidney’s brother and the killer.

00:30:15.169 --> 00:30:17.669
Let’s get into the Things You Missed.

00:30:17.669 --> 00:30:20.117
(knives slashing)

00:30:20.117 --> 00:30:22.710
♪ Dreadful Music ♪ 

00:30:22.710 --> 00:30:26.470
As with Screams 1 and 2, the opening shows
the slaying of a couple, only this time, we

00:30:26.470 --> 00:30:30.220
recognize one of the victims, it’s Cotton
Weary and his girlfriend. Cotton is complaining

00:30:30.220 --> 00:30:35.150
that he is only given a cameo in Stab 3, which
is basically his experience in Scream 3 -- despite

00:30:35.150 --> 00:30:38.340
being a big part of the second movie, they
kill him off right away in this one.

00:30:38.340 --> 00:30:41.820
One the criticisms of turning Scream into
a franchise was that it would become the very

00:30:41.820 --> 00:30:46.149
thing that the original Scream was satirizing,
and we see this intentionally, or not intentionally

00:30:46.149 --> 00:30:50.860
in Scream 3’s opening. In the last film,
Stab 1 attempted to recreate the opening of

00:30:50.860 --> 00:30:54.120
Scream, the death of Casey Becker. But certain
things are embellished for Hollywood, like

00:30:54.120 --> 00:30:57.580
how they add a shower scene which seems to
have no purpose other than showing a little

00:30:57.580 --> 00:30:58.580
skin.

00:30:58.580 --> 00:31:01.410
So Scream 3’s opening literally does that,
in the form of Cotton’s girlfriend getting

00:31:01.410 --> 00:31:04.750
out of the shower. There’s even a shot of
Christine’s towel dropping by her feet,

00:31:04.750 --> 00:31:08.780
very similar to Casey’s bathrobe in Stab.

00:31:08.780 --> 00:31:10.190
–[Sarah] The whole shower thing’s been done!
Vertigo. Hello?

00:31:10.190 --> 00:31:13.690
– It’s not Vertigo, it’s Psycho. As
we have discussed many times on this show.

00:31:13.690 --> 00:31:17.590
Then, Christine even channels her inner
Casey Becker herself when she kicks Ghostface

00:31:17.590 --> 00:31:18.590
to get away.

00:31:18.590 --> 00:31:21.600
Scream 2’s opening was all about the Stab
movie premiere, so there’s a slight nod

00:31:21.600 --> 00:31:25.220
to that as well, when Cotton is frantically
driving home, he nearly hits a bus with a

00:31:25.220 --> 00:31:26.840
Stab 2 advertisement on back.

00:31:26.840 --> 00:31:30.540
This next one might not be a reference, this
might just be Cotton being entirely focused

00:31:30.540 --> 00:31:34.500
on saving his girlfriend, but he leaves his
car door open as he goes inside, much like

00:31:34.500 --> 00:31:39.330
Bob Simms in Halloween, who was also probably
focused on one thing entirely. As you know

00:31:39.330 --> 00:31:42.650
if you’ve seen Scream, Halloween is basically
considered the big one, and is referenced

00:31:42.650 --> 00:31:46.529
all the time. But not that much in Scream
3. Although there is the one part where Sidney

00:31:46.529 --> 00:31:50.230
hears her mother’s voice telling her she’ll
protect her from the Boogeyman. The Boogeyman

00:31:50.230 --> 00:31:52.730
is a pseudonym for Michael Myers in the Halloween
films.

00:31:52.730 --> 00:31:56.669
Ghostface tries to pressure Cotton into revealing
the location of Sidney Prescott before offing

00:31:56.669 --> 00:32:00.770
him, and then we see Sidney living a secluded
life with her dog. By her door there’s a

00:32:00.770 --> 00:32:04.870
Fall of Troy poster advertising the play she
was in in college, Agamemnon. Does that mean

00:32:04.870 --> 00:32:07.940
she actually went through with being in the
play after defeating Mickey and Debbie on

00:32:07.940 --> 00:32:09.820
it’s stage? That’s dedication.

00:32:09.820 --> 00:32:13.140
She also continues to wear the Greek letters
given to her by her boyfriend when the murders

00:32:13.140 --> 00:32:16.049
were happening in the last movie. Can you
really blame her? He was the best guy she

00:32:16.049 --> 00:32:18.289
ever dated.
The next character we meet is Gale Weathers,

00:32:18.289 --> 00:32:22.960
who is giving a speech about being a journalist
lady. A woman in the crowd is wearing a #81

00:32:22.960 --> 00:32:28.279
jersey. Not many people know this, but Scream
1996 was not the first… or last… slasher

00:32:28.279 --> 00:32:32.541
movie to be called Scream. The original was
a 1981 film. I have not seen it, but according

00:32:32.541 --> 00:32:34.920
to the Letterboxd ratings, it is not very
good.

00:32:34.920 --> 00:32:38.929
This scene also has the best extra. This lady
is REALLY into Gale’s speech. This speech

00:32:38.929 --> 00:32:43.200
is part of a lecture series called FW Bestor
Lecture Series: Faces of Journalism, named

00:32:43.200 --> 00:32:45.870
after the movie’s art department coordinator
Claudia Bestor.

00:32:45.870 --> 00:32:49.779
Just like the last two movies, Sidney is at
home, when she sees bad news on the TV that

00:32:49.779 --> 00:32:51.559
involves her and her past.

00:32:51.559 --> 00:32:54.950
As we’re first introduced to Sunrise Studios,
where most of this movie takes place, we hear

00:32:54.950 --> 00:32:58.651
the song Red Right Hand by Nick Cave and the
Bad Seeds, which by this point is more or

00:32:58.651 --> 00:33:02.230
less the Scream theme song, this is our fourth
time hearing it, and the end credits would

00:33:02.230 --> 00:33:06.669
be the fifth, although that one is a special
Scream 3 version meant to close out the trilogy.

00:33:06.669 --> 00:33:10.210
Stab 3’s subtitle is “Return to Woodsboro”,
and this phrase keeps finding new meaning

00:33:10.210 --> 00:33:13.779
throughout the movie. It’s actually a leftover
from Kevin Williamson’s original outline

00:33:13.779 --> 00:33:17.600
for the script, that he planned after writing
Scream 2, but most of it was not used for

00:33:17.600 --> 00:33:21.830
what would become Scream 3. We would symbolically
return to Woodsboro immediately, because they’re

00:33:21.830 --> 00:33:25.860
shooting on the Woodsboro set. Sidney would
return to Woodsboro when she is later chased

00:33:25.860 --> 00:33:29.539
through this set. Later still, the focus of
the story is brought back to Woodsboro when

00:33:29.539 --> 00:33:33.220
we learn new information about the motivations
of Billy and Stu. And the movie would end

00:33:33.220 --> 00:33:37.350
with everyone returning Woodsboro, or at least
what appears to be Woodsboro to join Sidney

00:33:37.350 --> 00:33:41.670
at her secluded home. But this first scene
on the Woodsboro set contains more depth than

00:33:41.670 --> 00:33:44.300
what meets the eye.

00:33:44.300 --> 00:33:48.160
(Thunder)

00:33:48.160 --> 00:33:52.010
♪ Mysterious Music ♪

00:33:52.010 --> 00:33:55.570
When we meet Roman, the director of Stab 3,
he’s busy making a case for keeping the

00:33:55.570 --> 00:33:57.710
production going after Cotton’s death.

00:33:57.710 --> 00:34:00.900
– [Roman] Hire more security, hire the national
guard, just don’t kill the movie.

00:34:00.900 --> 00:34:04.520
– Which is funny, because he literally would
kill the movie, at least for the time being,

00:34:04.520 --> 00:34:08.889
by killing everyone in it. He’s seen arguing
with a studio executive played by Roger Corman,

00:34:08.889 --> 00:34:13.040
who tells him that violence in cinema is kind
of a no-no right now. Which is also a little

00:34:13.040 --> 00:34:16.579
joke, because Roger Corman is a well known
horror producer and director. He literally

00:34:16.579 --> 00:34:21.220
made movies such as Night of the Blood Beast.
A Bucket of Blood. Bloody Mama. Battle of

00:34:21.220 --> 00:34:24.720
Blood Island. Blood Bath. A remake of A Bucket
of Blood.

00:34:24.720 --> 00:34:29.810
But his concern over gory movies in 1999 was
very relevant, because Stab 3 is the in-universe

00:34:29.810 --> 00:34:34.110
stand-in for Scream 3, and Scream 3 was going
through the same problems at this time thanks

00:34:34.110 --> 00:34:37.350
to the Columbine Massacre, the worst high
school shooting of the millenium.

00:34:37.350 --> 00:34:41.329
The Columbine Massacre was carried out by
two high school seniors, and that is really

00:34:41.329 --> 00:34:44.970
only similarity to Scream that exists, but
that didn’t stop people who were already

00:34:44.970 --> 00:34:46.649
against horror movies.

00:34:46.649 --> 00:34:50.290
–[Wes] Everybody that didn’t like horror films
that was in a position of power, came after

00:34:50.290 --> 00:34:51.290
the studios.

00:34:51.290 --> 00:34:55.530
– [Zac] Columbine happened in April 1999.
Scream 3 started shooting in July, so there

00:34:55.530 --> 00:34:59.090
was a lot of pressure from the studio to limit
the gore, which is why we see less of it in

00:34:59.090 --> 00:35:03.080
this movie. According to Matthew Lillard,
he was already paid to appear in Scream 3

00:35:03.080 --> 00:35:08.079
as the returning villain Stu Macher, but Columbine
put an end to those plans. Wes Craven attempted

00:35:08.079 --> 00:35:11.910
to make sure the cultural context was not
forgotten, by sneaking in this article about

00:35:11.910 --> 00:35:15.360
Columbine into the scene where Sidney looks
through her file at the police station.

00:35:15.360 --> 00:35:19.520
The other articles in this file are just nonsense.
Look at this one. “The local police chief,

00:35:19.520 --> 00:35:23.079
the first official commissioned by the city
with the search committed in the name of the

00:35:23.079 --> 00:35:29.160
parents. The shadowy suspect, faces 121 charges
of murder, kidnapping, arms, smuggling, fraud

00:35:29.160 --> 00:35:33.030
and theft. 'His plea is he hadn’t committed
these offenses.' said the parents’ attorney,

00:35:33.030 --> 00:35:35.290
'we will find the Megan at all cost.'"

00:35:35.290 --> 00:35:39.540
When Gale, that is, the real Gale, shows up
on set, she’s hit by that wave of dreadful

00:35:39.540 --> 00:35:40.750
familiarity.

00:35:43.170 --> 00:35:45.579
–[Gale] Deja Voodoo.

00:35:45.579 --> 00:35:50.339
– Her misuse of the term déjà vu, right
outside of Sidney’s house, calls back to

00:35:50.339 --> 00:35:54.470
Sidney’s own misuse of the phrase in Scream
1. The nostalgia does not end there though,

00:35:54.470 --> 00:35:57.970
because the new Gale, aka Jennifer, is dressed
more like the old Gale.

00:35:57.970 --> 00:36:02.310
But what happens next is not all old and familiar.
Gale runs into Dewey, who has become an advisor

00:36:02.310 --> 00:36:06.020
for the movie. When these two met in Scream
2, Dewey was upset with how Gale portrayed

00:36:06.020 --> 00:36:10.290
him in her book. Now, Dewey has turned the
tables on her, and Gale is upset about how

00:36:10.290 --> 00:36:11.790
he’s portraying her in Stab 3.

00:36:11.790 --> 00:36:15.589
In my previous Scream analysis videos, I pointed
out Wes Craven’s easter eggs to his other

00:36:15.589 --> 00:36:18.960
major horror franchise, A Nightmare on Elm
Street. We don’t really get that much in

00:36:18.960 --> 00:36:22.280
the third one, but there is this one tiny
nightmare scene involving Sidney, where she

00:36:22.280 --> 00:36:23.750
sees her dead mother come to the window.

00:36:23.750 --> 00:36:26.990
If we’re being honest though, the reference
points a lot more towards Salem’s Lot than

00:36:26.990 --> 00:36:30.370
it does towards Freddy Krueger.
The next day, Sarah Darling, who plays the

00:36:30.370 --> 00:36:33.690
character Candy in Stab, has a meeting with
Roman at the production office.

00:36:33.690 --> 00:36:37.809
–[Sarah] I’m Candy, the chick who gets killed
second. I’m only in two scenes.

00:36:37.809 --> 00:36:40.880
– Like Cotton’s gripes about his character,
the complaint about her screen time could

00:36:40.880 --> 00:36:45.840
also apply to her role in Scream 3; she’s
only in 2 scenes. There’s also another 1:1

00:36:45.840 --> 00:36:49.210
parallel later, where the police mention that
there are three versions of the Stab 3 script,

00:36:49.210 --> 00:36:52.500
in order to try to keep the ending off the
internet -- the exact same approach used for

00:36:52.500 --> 00:36:56.710
Scream 3. And then there’s the romance between
Dewey and Gale. This time, rather than art

00:36:56.710 --> 00:37:00.870
imitating life, life would imitate art. These
two got married for realsies right before

00:37:00.870 --> 00:37:01.870
they started shooting.

00:37:01.870 --> 00:37:05.021
Anyway, during the line reading, Roman goes
off the script, and then claims there’s

00:37:05.021 --> 00:37:06.021
a new version.

00:37:06.021 --> 00:37:09.030
–[Roman] It’s not just a new script, it’s a
new movie.

00:37:09.030 --> 00:37:10.680
–[Sarah] What movie?
–[Roman] My movie.

00:37:10.680 --> 00:37:13.869
– Although we don’t know it yet, she’s
speaking to the real Roman, and he’s telling

00:37:13.869 --> 00:37:17.980
the truth. By killing the people involved
in Stab 3, he’s creating the lore for Stab

00:37:17.980 --> 00:37:19.770
3. He’s creating his movie.

00:37:19.770 --> 00:37:23.470
Candy is the successor to Tatum in Scream,
and her death closely resembles Tatum’s.

00:37:23.470 --> 00:37:27.830
Ghostface chases her into the prop room where,
among all the movie props, is a fridge. Remember,

00:37:27.830 --> 00:37:31.030
Tatum went to the garage to get drinks from
the fridge. She’s punched through a door

00:37:31.030 --> 00:37:34.150
where she’s left hanging on her stomach,
and this is where Ghostface get her. She dies

00:37:34.150 --> 00:37:37.089
in the exact same position that Tatum died
in in the pet flap.

00:37:37.089 --> 00:37:40.660
While Gale and Dewey are out to lunch to discuss
their past relationship, Gale emphasizes the

00:37:40.660 --> 00:37:44.589
importance of leaving him alone to go on the
sequel to 60 minutes, claiming she could have

00:37:44.589 --> 00:37:48.250
been the next Diane Sawyer. It’s a bit of
a Scream 2 reference, Diane Sawyer was the

00:37:48.250 --> 00:37:51.450
journalist that Cotton most wanted to be interviewed
on to gain fame.

00:37:51.450 --> 00:37:54.900
Back on the movie set, we have maybe our most
obvious set of crew reference easter eggs

00:37:54.900 --> 00:37:59.140
yet on the director’s chairs. Yes, they
are called that. This one’s for Peter Deming,

00:37:59.140 --> 00:38:03.400
the Director of Photography, this one belongs
to Sheila Waldron, the script supervisor and

00:38:03.400 --> 00:38:06.050
there’s one for Bruce Alan Miller, the production
designer.

00:38:06.050 --> 00:38:09.730
That evening, the cast gathers for the depressing
wrap party at Jennifer’s house, where the

00:38:09.730 --> 00:38:13.760
killing continue with her security guard,
and Roman continues to quote unquote write

00:38:13.760 --> 00:38:18.760
his movie by literally sending in script pages
telling them what to do via fax. Uhh, if you

00:38:18.760 --> 00:38:19.960
don’t know what fax is, look it up.

00:38:19.960 --> 00:38:24.740
The house blows up, taking out Tom, aka: the
new Dewey, and the old Gale punches the new

00:38:24.740 --> 00:38:28.750
Gale in the face, this is yet another twist
on the Scream tradition, of Ms. Weathers getting

00:38:28.750 --> 00:38:29.992
a facefull of knuckles in each film.

00:38:29.992 --> 00:38:34.080
The characters end up at the police station
where they’re finally reunited with Sidney.

00:38:34.080 --> 00:38:38.160
One nice detail at the station is the display
of movie posters and books about film. It’s

00:38:38.160 --> 00:38:42.290
like Randy suggested in Scream 1, that the
cops should study movies to save time.

00:38:42.290 --> 00:38:45.960
Speaking of Randy, he makes his return, in
the form of a post-mortem video message, which

00:38:45.960 --> 00:38:50.420
he would use to communicate a few new horror
movie rules.

00:38:50.420 --> 00:38:54.359
(cinematic impact)

00:38:54.359 --> 00:38:59.981
– [Randy] True trilogies are all about going
back to the beginning and discovering something

00:38:59.981 --> 00:39:04.780
that wasn’t true from the get-go. Godfather,
Jedi, all revealed something that we thought

00:39:04.780 --> 00:39:06.640
was true, that wasn’t true.

00:39:06.640 --> 00:39:09.970
– This was not his first time referencing
the original Star Wars trilogy, we saw him

00:39:09.970 --> 00:39:13.220
mention it in Scream 2, and it wouldn't be
long before it came up again.

00:39:13.220 --> 00:39:16.910
The two Gales meet the Sunrise Studios archivist,
who is played by Carrie Fisher,

00:39:16.910 --> 00:39:19.820
aka, Princess Leia in Star Wars.

00:39:19.820 --> 00:39:25.540
–[Bianca] I was up for Princess Leia. I was this
close. So who gets it? The one who sleeps

00:39:25.540 --> 00:39:26.540
with George Lucas.

00:39:26.540 --> 00:39:30.339
– She also shares something in common with
Sidney, her trilogy also involves the surprise

00:39:30.339 --> 00:39:32.819
twist where she discovers someone is her brother.

00:39:32.819 --> 00:39:36.420
They pull out the file for Maureen Prescott,
who at the time was going by Rina Reynolds.

00:39:36.420 --> 00:39:40.250
At the bottom of her resume, we can see that
she started her acting career in theatre,

00:39:40.250 --> 00:39:43.930
just as her daughter would go on to do. One
of the plays she was in was even called I

00:39:43.930 --> 00:39:48.120
Want To Scream. So they’ve got a whole little
family involved in the dramatic arts. Maureen

00:39:48.120 --> 00:39:52.680
with her acting in plays and horror films;
Roman, the director; and Sidney, the live

00:39:52.680 --> 00:39:53.680
performer.

00:39:53.680 --> 00:39:55.890
If Candy’s death was a callback to Tatum,
this scene where Sidney is chased through

00:39:55.890 --> 00:39:59.790
the movie set is a repeat of her own scene
in Scream, right down to her screwing herself

00:39:59.790 --> 00:40:03.660
by locking herself in with Ghostface at the
front door. There’s also another similarity.

00:40:03.660 --> 00:40:07.380
In Scream, she calls the police using this
program on her computer, and Dewey immediately

00:40:07.380 --> 00:40:11.590
shows up at her door. In Scream 3, she literally
just yells out for Dewey and he comes running

00:40:11.590 --> 00:40:13.060
because he’s already at the studio.

00:40:13.060 --> 00:40:16.890
The cops show up and we see this one new cop
named “J West”. This is the last crew

00:40:16.890 --> 00:40:20.060
reference easter egg, it’s a reference to
the assistant production coordinator John

00:40:20.060 --> 00:40:21.220
Burton West.

00:40:21.220 --> 00:40:24.640
After discovering Maureen worked in the movies,
Dewey and the Gales confront the producer

00:40:24.640 --> 00:40:28.109
John Milton, who basically tells them that
he held these parties back in the day where

00:40:28.109 --> 00:40:31.910
industry bigwigs took advantage of actresses,
and he basically turned a blind eye to this

00:40:31.910 --> 00:40:32.910
illegal activity.

00:40:32.910 --> 00:40:37.079
If you’ve followed the entertainment industry
recently, this might sound familiar. In 2018

00:40:37.079 --> 00:40:40.990
film producer Harvey Weinstein was arrested
as many allegations came out against him,

00:40:40.990 --> 00:40:45.830
some of which dated back to the 1970s, the
very same time period that John Milton claimed

00:40:45.830 --> 00:40:49.440
to have these parties. Could this be a coincidence?
Maybe not.

00:40:49.440 --> 00:40:52.460
From my understanding, Weinstein got away
with this for as long as he did because he

00:40:52.460 --> 00:40:56.359
targeted small time actresses. They did not
take legal action because they were afraid

00:40:56.359 --> 00:41:00.380
they’d be blacklisted from the industry,
since Harvey had contacts with all of Hollywood.

00:41:00.380 --> 00:41:04.160
For example, two actresses up for parts in
Lord of the Rings lost their roles simply

00:41:04.160 --> 00:41:07.260
because Weinstein told Peter Jackson that
they were a “nightmare to work with.”

00:41:07.260 --> 00:41:09.200
That’s the kind of influence that he had.

00:41:09.200 --> 00:41:12.670
But with the advent of the internet and social
media, the victims were able to discover that

00:41:12.670 --> 00:41:17.330
they were not the only ones. There were a
lot. Like a whole lot. So they came together,

00:41:17.330 --> 00:41:20.890
knowing that they couldn’t ALL be blacklisted.
The point of all this is, while they didn’t

00:41:20.890 --> 00:41:24.360
get him arrested for many years, there were
rumors about Weinstein’s behavior. So it’s

00:41:24.360 --> 00:41:28.140
very possible that Wes Craven may have known
about these rumors and pulled the biggest

00:41:28.140 --> 00:41:32.500
power move of all time, by putting a Harvey
Weinstein character in his movie. Which by

00:41:32.500 --> 00:41:35.839
the way was produced by Bob and Harvey Weinstein.

00:41:35.839 --> 00:41:40.090
But unlike these relatively unknown actresses,
Wes Craven had already made it in Hollywood.

00:41:40.090 --> 00:41:43.710
Weinstein couldn’t do anything to him, and
besides, he was already nearing the end of

00:41:43.710 --> 00:41:47.700
his career. And despite being the executive
producer, Harvey Weinstein couldn’t really

00:41:47.700 --> 00:41:52.430
stop Wes from using Scream 3 to make this
indirect accusation. Because if he order Wes

00:41:52.430 --> 00:41:55.440
to change the John Milton scene, he’s just
gonna look guilty.

00:41:55.440 --> 00:41:58.700
According to the film’s editor, Patrick
Lussier, in an article on Slate, the only

00:41:58.700 --> 00:42:02.331
note to come from the Weinstein brothers at
all, came from Harvey’s brother, Bob, he

00:42:02.331 --> 00:42:06.050
just had a note about the ending, which I’ll
get back to later in this video. So Wes had

00:42:06.050 --> 00:42:09.810
his boss backed into a corner where he could
not give any notes. It’s got to be one of

00:42:09.810 --> 00:42:14.910
the most legendary moves that Wes has ever
made if this, in fact, is not a total, huge

00:42:14.910 --> 00:42:15.910
coincidence.

00:42:15.910 --> 00:42:17.700
–[Exec] Hollywood is full of criminals whose careers
are flourishing.

00:42:17.700 --> 00:42:18.780
–[Roman] I'm not a criminal!

00:42:18.780 --> 00:42:22.359
– Yeah, I don’t think it is. And to further
add to the suspicion, some of the accusers

00:42:22.359 --> 00:42:26.309
are connected to the Scream franchise. Rosanna
Arquette is the real life sister of David

00:42:26.309 --> 00:42:30.760
Arquette, Dewey. And Rose McGowan, who plays
Tatum in Scream, is the onscreen sister of

00:42:30.760 --> 00:42:34.910
David Arquette. Angelina Jolie, potentially
has two characters named after her in Scream 3

00:42:34.910 --> 00:42:38.560
Jennifer Jolie and Angelina Tyler who says
this:

00:42:38.560 --> 00:42:45.609
–[Angelina] I did not f*ck that pig Milton to get
a leading role just to die here with second-rate

00:42:45.609 --> 00:42:46.760
celebrities like you two!

00:42:46.760 --> 00:42:49.750
– The movie’s climax takes place at a
mansion party where Roman’s birthday is

00:42:49.750 --> 00:42:52.711
to be celebrated. While we don’t realize
it at the time, I don’t think it’s any

00:42:52.711 --> 00:42:56.580
coincidence that Roman puts his plan into
action on his birthday, the day he became

00:42:56.580 --> 00:42:58.490
Maureen Prescott’s bastard child.

00:42:58.490 --> 00:43:02.099
The party splits up to explore the mansion
and Jennifer finds herself stuck in a passage

00:43:02.099 --> 00:43:05.839
with Ghostface. She sees Dewey on the other
side of this soundproof glass barrier and

00:43:05.839 --> 00:43:09.640
struggles to get his attention. This is the
same situation that the real Gale was in in

00:43:09.640 --> 00:43:12.910
Scream 2, when she was unable to communicate
with him in the recording studio.

00:43:12.910 --> 00:43:16.590
There's another repeat of a Scream 2 situation
in the basement when Roman is knocked out

00:43:16.590 --> 00:43:20.520
after falling down the stairs, but his body
blocks the only exit. This is like when Sidney

00:43:20.520 --> 00:43:23.210
had to climb over Mickey to escape the crashed
car.

00:43:23.210 --> 00:43:27.210
Roman uses his capture of Gale and Dewey to
lure Sidney into the house, but she's prepared

00:43:27.210 --> 00:43:31.220
and greets him with her hidden gun. Unfortunately
for her, this is the one time that she didn't

00:43:31.220 --> 00:43:34.490
deliver a follow up shot to the head and it
comes back back bite her.

00:43:34.490 --> 00:43:38.500
The final confrontation takes place in the
secret screening room where Roman was theoretically

00:43:38.500 --> 00:43:41.900
conceived. After revealing himself as the
killer there are a couple more parallels to

00:43:41.900 --> 00:43:46.140
Scream 1, the bringing out of a gagged victim
and Roman cocking his head just like Ghostface

00:43:46.140 --> 00:43:48.720
when he first pinned down Sidney at her dad’s
house.

00:43:48.720 --> 00:43:52.140
After he is defeated, we’re treated to an
epilogue. We see Sidney essentially let her

00:43:52.140 --> 00:43:56.570
guard down for the first time since that fateful
phone call a few years ago. Then we see Detective

00:43:56.570 --> 00:44:00.920
Kincaid, the cop from LA, who apparently came
back with her? He’s holding a bowl of popcorn

00:44:00.920 --> 00:44:05.099
and says they’re about to start a movie.
So the Scream trilogy ends just as it began,

00:44:05.099 --> 00:44:06.540
with popcorn and a movie.

00:44:06.540 --> 00:44:10.101
I personally think this was supposed to be
the end of the series. By comparing the last

00:44:10.101 --> 00:44:13.670
ever Scream scene to the first ever, we see
the changed state of the world, before and

00:44:13.670 --> 00:44:17.099
after the defeat of Roman, the driving force
behind all of the bad things that happened

00:44:17.099 --> 00:44:22.819
to Sidney in the 1990s. However, having only
one killer doesn’t follow the Scream formula.

00:44:22.819 --> 00:44:29.680
There could be one more villain from the production
of Stab 3 who has yet to be taken care of.

00:44:29.680 --> 00:44:31.820
(cinematic impact)

00:44:31.820 --> 00:44:36.100
♪ Upbeat Music ♪

00:44:36.100 --> 00:44:41.340
Earlier in this video I mentioned two darker
topics, Weinstein and Columbine. I stated

00:44:41.340 --> 00:44:45.290
that Wes Craven and his editor only received
one note on the film and it came from the

00:44:45.290 --> 00:44:49.220
innocent of the two brothers, Bob Weinstein.
His idea was to remove the second Ghostface

00:44:49.220 --> 00:44:50.540
to just focus on Roman.

00:44:50.540 --> 00:44:54.520
The reason for this, or at least one of the
reasons, may have been teased in the film.

00:44:54.520 --> 00:44:57.619
Remember the scene where the producers are
having a discussion with Roman about the future

00:44:57.619 --> 00:45:01.761
of the film, emphasizing that stringed instruments
in cinema are a big deal right now? A big

00:45:01.761 --> 00:45:05.710
reason for that was Columbine. So the shooting
may have also been part of the reason that

00:45:05.710 --> 00:45:08.390
they moved away from the initial concept of
having two killers.

00:45:08.390 --> 00:45:12.400
The accomplice was going to be Angelina Tyler,
the woman who was set to play the fictional

00:45:12.400 --> 00:45:16.599
version of Sidney in Stab 3. You can still
see remnants of this storyline in the film.

00:45:16.599 --> 00:45:21.109
In fact, some fans believe that Angelina is
still canonically the 6th Ghostface, and she

00:45:21.109 --> 00:45:23.559
was killed before she ever got the chance
to reveal herself.

00:45:23.559 --> 00:45:27.329
Her story is that she won the role in a talent
search, but we later find she did this by

00:45:27.329 --> 00:45:29.170
sleeping with the producer.

00:45:29.170 --> 00:45:31.019
– [Tom] You gotta be praying this movie
keeps going.

00:45:31.019 --> 00:45:32.770
–[Angelina] Of course I am, but not at the expense

00:45:32.770 --> 00:45:33.770
of people’s lives.

00:45:33.770 --> 00:45:36.069
– [Zac] You either die a hero or live --

00:45:36.069 --> 00:45:39.720
There are three main sequences that make Angelina
look guilty. The first is shortly after Jennifer’s

00:45:39.720 --> 00:45:43.410
house explodes. She briefly disappears, and
comes down the hill well after everyone else,

00:45:43.410 --> 00:45:47.190
almost like she knew where to jump to avoid
taking fall damage. This allows her to completely

00:45:47.190 --> 00:45:50.940
miss the altercation with Roman. Dewey asks
her about her whereabouts, and she never actually

00:45:50.940 --> 00:45:52.040
answers the question.

00:45:52.040 --> 00:45:54.520
– [Angelina] Tom was in the house!

00:45:54.520 --> 00:45:57.390
Then, there’s the bathroom scene, which
by the way is setup exactly like the school

00:45:57.390 --> 00:45:58.390
bathroom scene in

00:45:58.390 --> 00:46:02.470
Scream 1. Sidney discovers her seemingly changing
into the Ghostface costume, but Angelina plays

00:46:02.470 --> 00:46:06.310
it off as her wanting to take props as souvenirs
now that the movie is shut down. But what

00:46:06.310 --> 00:46:10.100
if this is actually her getting caught, and,
being the actress that she is, talking her

00:46:10.100 --> 00:46:11.100
way out of it?

00:46:11.100 --> 00:46:14.550
This could also be the reason that Roman decides
to kill her off. He probably figured that

00:46:14.550 --> 00:46:18.000
Sidney knew she was guilty, and it was better
to get rid of her before she had the chance

00:46:18.000 --> 00:46:21.400
to be arrested and interrogated, where she
could possibly give them both away.

00:46:21.400 --> 00:46:24.790
He knew Sidney wouldn’t accuse her immediately
though, because she wouldn’t want a repeat

00:46:24.790 --> 00:46:28.040
of Cotton Weary on her conscience, who went
to jail because he was wrongfully accused

00:46:28.040 --> 00:46:30.900
by Sid when she wasn’t quite 100% sure.

00:46:30.900 --> 00:46:34.819
So I think Angelina makes up an excuse, quickly
scurries away, and goes into the sound stage

00:46:34.819 --> 00:46:38.220
to finish putting on the Ghostface costume.
When Sidney goes into the movie set version

00:46:38.220 --> 00:46:41.710
of her room, there are clearly two killers
here, someone is pushing on the door, and

00:46:41.710 --> 00:46:45.011
then 3 seconds later Ghostface is all the
way on the other side of the room, behind

00:46:45.011 --> 00:46:46.011
her in the window.

00:46:46.011 --> 00:46:49.410
I think the one in the window is Angelina,
because the shoes we see during the struggle

00:46:49.410 --> 00:46:52.550
are the exact same as the ones that Sidney
noticed under the bathroom stall. When the

00:46:52.550 --> 00:46:57.390
lights go on, we get a better look at Angelina
wearing these shoes with a black… Skirt?

00:46:57.390 --> 00:47:01.800
Uh... I don’t know the terminology for this
piece of clothing, but the loose black fabric

00:47:01.800 --> 00:47:04.329
could easily help her blend into the Ghostface
cloak.

00:47:04.329 --> 00:47:08.260
And the third sequence where she looks guilty
is Roman’s birthday party. Sure, they appear

00:47:08.260 --> 00:47:12.530
to be using the buddy system for safety, but
Roman and Angelina are each one-on-one with

00:47:12.530 --> 00:47:13.530
a victim.

00:47:13.530 --> 00:47:16.960
Dewey and Gale also poke around and find a
Ghostface mask, cell phone and voice changer,

00:47:16.960 --> 00:47:21.089
in a closet. But these are not Roman’s because
he clearly has his own. I can understand having

00:47:21.089 --> 00:47:23.950
a backup costume, but why have a second voice
changer?

00:47:23.950 --> 00:47:27.370
Angelina was supposed to be with Tyson, but
she disappears, and Roman fakes his death,

00:47:27.370 --> 00:47:30.700
just like Billy did. In hindsight, he’s
probably the one who taught Billy to make

00:47:30.700 --> 00:47:32.840
that Hollywood level corn syrup mixture.

00:47:32.840 --> 00:47:36.450
During the big reveal, Roman even suggests
that he gave Billy pointers on being a killer,

00:47:36.450 --> 00:47:40.110
such as having a partner to sell out once
you get caught. This means Roman probably

00:47:40.110 --> 00:47:42.010
also had a disposable partner.

00:47:42.010 --> 00:47:45.500
When we last see Angelina, she’s frantically
trying to leave the house. Could it be that

00:47:45.500 --> 00:47:49.410
at this point, she knows Roman is done with
her, and she’s now fearing for her life?

00:47:49.410 --> 00:47:52.890
Or is this just part of the act? We don’t
actually see her die, she just gets stabbed

00:47:52.890 --> 00:47:54.560
in the shoulder, and dragged out of view.

00:47:54.560 --> 00:47:59.490
It is awfully inconclusive for a Scream character
to go out like this. Maybe Scream 5 will feature

00:47:59.490 --> 00:48:01.650
Stu and Angelina as the Ghostface killers.

00:48:01.650 --> 00:48:04.990
Personally, I don’t think there’s a ton
of evidence to suggest she’s alive, though

00:48:04.990 --> 00:48:09.089
it is definitely a small possibility. But
I am of the belief that she was the Roman’s

00:48:09.089 --> 00:48:11.940
accomplice, and there’s a ton of evidence
to support that. Let me know your thoughts

00:48:11.940 --> 00:48:13.920
on Angelina in the comments.

00:48:13.920 --> 00:48:19.520
Scream 3 was marketed as the last Scream.
The final act. The very end. So join me next

00:48:19.520 --> 00:48:23.704
time on Things You Missed as I cover Scre4m.
Also known as Scream 4.

00:48:23.704 --> 00:48:27.849
Scre4m welcomed us back to Woodsboro High
School, it doesn’t look exactly the same,

00:48:27.849 --> 00:48:30.760
but this could technically be a different
entrance, and this could just be a different

00:48:30.760 --> 00:48:34.040
part of the building. But one aspect that
is definitely a new addition, which you might

00:48:34.040 --> 00:48:37.780
have missed, is this bust, dedicated to the
late Principal Arthur Himbry, who was one

00:48:37.780 --> 00:48:39.150
of the victims in Scream 1.

00:48:39.150 --> 00:48:41.859
Here’s a little bit of a better look from
a behind the scenes photo.

00:48:41.859 --> 00:48:45.430
For other references you might not have noticed,
like the movie props featured in the background

00:48:45.430 --> 00:48:47.571
of Stab-a-thon, stick around to the end of
this video.

00:48:47.571 --> 00:48:54.300
This video is sponsored by NordVPN

00:48:54.300 --> 00:48:57.500
♪ Metal Music ♪

00:48:57.500 --> 00:49:01.020
Welcome to Things You Missed. I am ‘Don’t
Look At My Tits I Have A Mind Morris,’ and

00:49:01.020 --> 00:49:04.670
if you remembered that reference, it probably
hasn’t been long since you last watched

00:49:04.670 --> 00:49:08.500
Scre4m. And I actually think this is one of
the most warranted examples putting a number

00:49:08.500 --> 00:49:13.849
in the title in place of a letter. In a movie
like Se7en, or Thir13n Ghosts, there’s no

00:49:13.849 --> 00:49:18.220
real reason for it other than style. But since
this movie serves as a satire of horror movie

00:49:18.220 --> 00:49:21.690
remakes, most of which have the same title
as the original, inserting the number 4 allows

00:49:21.690 --> 00:49:26.190
you to read it as just Scream, while still
differentiating that this is the fourth movie.

00:49:26.190 --> 00:49:29.310
Which makes it all the more disappointing
that they didn’t follow suit with Scream

00:49:29.310 --> 00:49:34.869
(2022) by calling it 5cream. Let’s get in
to the Things You Missed.

00:49:34.869 --> 00:49:39.160
♪ Mysterious Music ♪

00:49:39.160 --> 00:49:43.079
Being a remake, this one opens exactly the
same way the original did, with a teen girl

00:49:43.079 --> 00:49:46.270
receiving a phone call. Sherrie and Trudie
are trying to pick a movie, and we see the

00:49:46.270 --> 00:49:49.799
blue screen on the TV, something I talked
about when I covered the original Scream,

00:49:49.799 --> 00:49:53.770
which represents the idea that a movie is
about to begin, not only for us, the audience,

00:49:53.770 --> 00:49:57.060
but also the characters, who are somewhat
aware that they are living through a movie.

00:49:57.060 --> 00:50:00.960
However, this is the first movie to take place
well after the others, it’s set in 2010,

00:50:00.960 --> 00:50:04.609
so the new horror reference points for the
young characters are noughties horror like

00:50:04.609 --> 00:50:09.280
Saw and Final Destination. Saw in particular
seems to have the biggest influence on Scream 4.

00:50:09.280 --> 00:50:12.970
It’s the movie that Sherrie chooses for
movie night, and the color grade takes after

00:50:12.970 --> 00:50:16.890
the Saw look. For example, look at the first
outdoor scene, and how the bloom resembles

00:50:16.890 --> 00:50:21.319
the look of the Public Execution Trap from
Saw 7. Or for a better idea of exactly how

00:50:21.319 --> 00:50:24.640
the look of this movie differs from the previous
ones, try comparing the Stab footage from

00:50:24.640 --> 00:50:26.480
Scream 2 to that of the fourth movie.

00:50:26.480 --> 00:50:30.480
There are also a few scenes that seem to have
Saw’s imprint on them. Olivia encounters

00:50:30.480 --> 00:50:34.500
her masked attacker hiding in her closet,
just like Adam from Saw. Rebecca is ambushed

00:50:34.500 --> 00:50:38.170
in an empty parking garage, just like Dr.
Gordon from Saw. One of the side characters

00:50:38.170 --> 00:50:42.490
is Robbie Mercer, who is played by Erik Knudsen;
he was Daniel Matthews in Saw 2, and both

00:50:42.490 --> 00:50:46.790
of them know a lot about cameras. Stab is
now a seven movie series, just like Saw was

00:50:46.790 --> 00:50:51.599
at the time. Stab-a-thon is held in an abandoned
barn. This was well before Jigsaw, the Saw

00:50:51.599 --> 00:50:55.420
movie set in an abandoned barn, but Gale’s
coming across this creepy pig head next to

00:50:55.420 --> 00:50:59.330
these rusty gears still looks like something
right out of the original Saw saga, or as

00:50:59.330 --> 00:51:01.920
some of you suggested on my Saw videos, the
Saw-ga.

00:51:01.920 --> 00:51:05.650
The opening of Scre4m also serves as a mission
statement about what we should expect for

00:51:05.650 --> 00:51:09.420
this movie. And that is, to expect the unexpected.

00:51:09.420 --> 00:51:14.431
– [Charlie] The unexpected is the new cliche.
Modern audiences get savvy to the rules of

00:51:14.431 --> 00:51:17.450
the originals. So the reverse has become the
new standard.

00:51:17.450 --> 00:51:21.059
– The intro does this with it’s false
openings. First we see two girls get killed;

00:51:21.059 --> 00:51:25.160
turns out that’s just Stab 6. Then we get
some meta-cinema commentary from our new characters…

00:51:25.160 --> 00:51:29.180
–[Rachel] It’s all so predictable, there’s no
element of surprise, you can see everything

00:51:29.180 --> 00:51:30.959
coming! (stab)

00:51:30.959 --> 00:51:35.190
– …but that just turns out to be Stab
7. So we’re seeing first hand how filmmakers

00:51:35.190 --> 00:51:39.160
are now actively trying to do what the audience
doesn’t expect in the decade since Stab 3.

00:51:39.160 --> 00:51:44.500
And this, of course, also applies to our
movie, Scream 4. Scre4m.

00:51:44.500 --> 00:51:48.760
So after those two false intros, we meet our
real intro characters, Marnie Cooper and Jenny

00:51:48.760 --> 00:51:52.910
Randall. Marnie does share a last name with
Cici Cooper from Scream 2, but they probably

00:51:52.910 --> 00:51:56.100
aren’t related -- despite their shared eye
and hair color. I’m actually more interested

00:51:56.100 --> 00:52:00.610
in her first name, Marnie, because it brings
to mind the 1964 Alfred Hitchcock film. Being

00:52:00.610 --> 00:52:03.819
one of the most important figures in the horror
genre, Hitchcock has already been the subject

00:52:03.819 --> 00:52:07.849
of several homages in the Scream franchise,
like Billy Loomis sharing a name with Sam

00:52:07.849 --> 00:52:12.300
Loomis from Psycho, the shower shot from Psycho
being in Stab, and one of the actresses in

00:52:12.300 --> 00:52:16.049
Scream 3 incorrectly identifying it as a reference
to Vertigo, among others.

00:52:16.049 --> 00:52:19.940
In the classroom where Cinema Club is held,
you can spot a Vertigo poster. On the chalkboard,

00:52:19.940 --> 00:52:23.390
there’s a lesson about the plot structure
of Hitchcock’s Rear Window, and Kirby’s

00:52:23.390 --> 00:52:27.790
room has a Rear Window poster as well. Marnie
may not be the only Hitchcock namesake. Sidney

00:52:27.790 --> 00:52:32.589
has a publicist named Rebecca, which could
be an homage to Hitchcock’s 1940 film Rebecca,

00:52:32.589 --> 00:52:36.359
and the police officer, Perkins, is actually
named Anthony Perkins, we hear his partner

00:52:36.359 --> 00:52:37.830
call him that.

00:52:37.830 --> 00:52:39.000
–[Det Hoss] Anthony! Perkins!

00:52:39.000 --> 00:52:42.670
– Anthony Perkins is the actor who
played Norman Bates in Psycho. The rest of

00:52:42.670 --> 00:52:46.760
the intro sequence features a lot of fun homages
to the Scream franchise, like Ghostface trying

00:52:46.760 --> 00:52:48.860
to tell Jenny to think of him as her director.

00:52:48.860 --> 00:52:52.089
– [Caller] You’re in my movie. You’ve
got a fun part! So don't blow it.

00:52:52.089 --> 00:52:55.890
– The last villain literally was the
director of Stab 3. Marnie is sent crashing

00:52:55.890 --> 00:52:58.040
through the glass door a la Steve Orth from
Scream.

00:52:58.040 --> 00:52:59.040
– [Caller] But you? You’re the dumb blond
with the big tits. We’ll have some fun with

00:52:59.040 --> 00:53:04.410
you before you die.

00:53:04.410 --> 00:53:07.339
– And just like Sidney predicted in the
first Scream, she makes the mistake of running

00:53:07.339 --> 00:53:11.420
up the stairs rather than out the door. She
ends up going to the attic, like Sidney did

00:53:11.420 --> 00:53:14.369
while trying to escape Ghostface at Stu’s
house, and then eventually to the garage,

00:53:14.369 --> 00:53:18.340
where her escape is foiled by the garage door,
just like Tatum in Scream.

00:53:18.340 --> 00:53:22.700
Dewey is woken up by his ringtone.

00:53:22.700 --> 00:53:28.420
(80s techno)

00:53:28.420 --> 00:53:32.849
The song is Axel F, from the soundtrack of
Beverly Hills Cop. And Dewey is also a cop

00:53:32.849 --> 00:53:36.559
again, he’s not the Sheriff of Woodsboro.
Also of note, this song has been remade many

00:53:36.559 --> 00:53:40.600
times, by the likes of TechnoCop, Clock, and
yes… Crazy Frog.

00:53:40.600 --> 00:53:41.600
–[Crazy Frog] Ding. Ding.

00:53:41.600 --> 00:53:45.069
– Soon we meet the next generation
of Woodsboro High School students. It seems

00:53:45.069 --> 00:53:48.569
that Kirby knows who the killer is without
actually knowing who the killer is.

00:53:48.569 --> 00:53:51.089
–[Kirby] Before you get in the car, you have to
promise not to kill me.

00:53:51.089 --> 00:53:54.200
– There’s a similar foreshadowing moment
like this later when Officer Hoss is doing

00:53:54.200 --> 00:53:57.980
a crossword puzzle and asks Perkins for a
four letter word for courage.

00:53:57.980 --> 00:54:00.819
– [Perkins] Uhh… Guts.

00:54:00.819 --> 00:54:04.700
– It would not be long after that when Olivia’s
guts would be ripped out by Ghostface. The

00:54:04.700 --> 00:54:08.740
event is also foreshadowed by these chinese
take-out boxes that have a red splatter design.

00:54:08.740 --> 00:54:12.060
But before we get there, we would check in
on the current state of Gale Riley, whose

00:54:12.060 --> 00:54:15.067
office plays host to a few more Things You
Missed.

00:54:15.067 --> 00:54:18.638
(Thunder)

00:54:23.578 --> 00:54:26.867
Each new character in the fourth installment
of Scream is a spiritual successor to one

00:54:26.867 --> 00:54:30.751
of the characters from the original Scream.
Which is why it’s a satire of horror movie remakes.

00:54:30.751 --> 00:54:34.751
Like a remake you have new actors
playing old roles. Jill is the new Sidney

00:54:34.751 --> 00:54:37.917
Prescott, a high school student thrust into
danger due to the attack of her family member.

00:54:37.917 --> 00:54:42.400
Trevor is Billy Loomis, the boyfriend who
becomes a major suspect. Kirby is Randy Meeks,

00:54:42.400 --> 00:54:45.890
the biggest horror film buff of the friend
group and walking knowledge database. Olivia

00:54:45.890 --> 00:54:50.609
is Tatum Riley, the attractive popular girl
who dies way too early. Charlie is Stu Macher,

00:54:50.609 --> 00:54:55.060
the easily peer pressured friend. Robbie Mercer
is Kenny, the camera guy. And Rebecca is Gale

00:54:55.060 --> 00:54:59.559
Weathers, the media. She even makes the comparison
between Dewey and Barney Fife, just like Gale

00:54:59.559 --> 00:55:01.640
did back in Scream 2.

00:55:01.640 --> 00:55:09.240
– [Dewey] Page 32. Deputy Dewey filled the
room with his Barney Fife-ish presence.

00:55:09.240 --> 00:55:12.230
– [Rebecca] Just wait, Barney Fife, I’m
running an event here.

00:55:12.230 --> 00:55:14.799
– Speaking of Gale, she’s now writing
a work of fiction, after penning the book

00:55:14.799 --> 00:55:18.319
trilogy that became the basis for the Stab
movies, and we can see them right here: The

00:55:18.319 --> 00:55:22.010
Woodsboro Murders, College Terror and Hollywood
Horror. It seems that she also wrote other

00:55:22.010 --> 00:55:26.220
stories about a Ghostface killer because there’s
this additional book, Ghostface Returns, which

00:55:26.220 --> 00:55:29.700
we get a better look at later in Jill’s
room. This is probably the basis of the fictional

00:55:29.700 --> 00:55:31.519
Stab movies 4 through 7.

00:55:31.519 --> 00:55:34.590
After news that the killings have started
again breaks, we see the Woodsboro police

00:55:34.590 --> 00:55:38.809
station, and if you pause, you can see the
KQIS 6 van whirring by in the foreground.

00:55:38.809 --> 00:55:42.369
This is the same station that Gale worked
for back in Scream 1, where the van was parked

00:55:42.369 --> 00:55:46.609
outside during Stu Macher’s party. We also
see the channel 6 microphone appear a couple

00:55:46.609 --> 00:55:47.609
times throughout the movie.

00:55:47.609 --> 00:55:51.370
Rebecca is celebrating the news of the two
girls being butchered, and she calls it a

00:55:51.370 --> 00:55:54.680
payday. The media sensationalizing the crimes
has been a theme that has run through each

00:55:54.680 --> 00:55:58.460
of the Scream movies so far. In the first
and second films it’s the TV news media,

00:55:58.460 --> 00:56:02.010
in the third it’s the Hollywood film industry
and now we’re seeing it happen in publishing.

00:56:02.010 --> 00:56:05.780
There would also be another angle to this
present in Scream 4: social media. Robbie

00:56:05.780 --> 00:56:09.691
gets involved as essentially what we would
now call the IRL streamer, and the killers

00:56:09.691 --> 00:56:13.539
are recording all their kills, which Jill
plans to use to help frame Trevor and Charlie

00:56:13.539 --> 00:56:17.309
and anticipates that the footage will go viral
and garner her more attention. However, she

00:56:17.309 --> 00:56:19.450
fails, so this part never pans out.

00:56:19.450 --> 00:56:22.010
Later in the movie, Rebecca tries to convince
Sidney to sign a new book deal.

00:56:22.010 --> 00:56:26.360
–[Rebecca] You’re a victim. For life. So embrace
it!

00:56:26.360 --> 00:56:29.930
– And this is another aspect of the social
commentary about the media. Rebecca doesn't

00:56:29.930 --> 00:56:33.960
understand the point of Sidney's book, it's
about her journey to no longer be the victim.

00:56:33.960 --> 00:56:36.470
Jill, on the other hand, is on a journey to
become the victim.

00:56:36.470 --> 00:56:42.079
–[Jill] How do you think people become famous
anymore? You don’t have to achieve anything.

00:56:42.079 --> 00:56:46.869
You just gotta have f*cked up sh*t happen
to you.

00:56:46.869 --> 00:56:50.790
– She's an extreme example of someone with
what we would now call victim mentality. Like

00:56:50.790 --> 00:56:54.579
the original Scream's social commentary about
sensationalizing violence in the news, and

00:56:54.579 --> 00:56:58.869
Scream 3's commentary about inappropriate
relations in Hollywood, Scre4m's take on victim

00:56:58.869 --> 00:57:02.910
mentality has only become more relevant over
the years as more and more people try to invent

00:57:02.910 --> 00:57:06.420
a scenario where they are perceived as having
been wronged, with the intention of going

00:57:06.420 --> 00:57:07.420
viral.

00:57:07.420 --> 00:57:11.240
The "my experience with ____" trope on social
media has become as cliche as the slasher

00:57:11.240 --> 00:57:12.910
movies that Randy idolized in Scream.

00:57:12.910 --> 00:57:17.990
–[sunhatkid] He had me unfollow all of them, which
to me was really weird, and I didn’t like it.

00:57:18.990 --> 00:57:21.710
– But anyway, when Rebecca meets Gale, Gale
tells her that she is Gale Riley now; she’s

00:57:21.710 --> 00:57:24.579
been married to Dewey for 10 years, to which
Rebecca is surprised.

00:57:24.579 --> 00:57:27.230
– It always seemed like more of a movie
romance than a real one because it was a movie.

00:57:27.230 --> 00:57:31.160
And in real life you two would never be…
ooh!

00:57:31.160 --> 00:57:34.369
– In my Scream 3 video, I mentioned that
Dewey and Gale’s marriage was one of the

00:57:34.369 --> 00:57:38.570
many plot elements inspired by real life,
because David Arquette and Courtney Cox actually

00:57:38.570 --> 00:57:43.200
got married. Now they had actually been married
10 years and, like in the movie, their relationship

00:57:43.200 --> 00:57:47.560
was in trouble. They separated in real life
in 2010, the same year this movie is set.

00:57:47.560 --> 00:57:51.260
The trouble in the on-screen marriage is exemplified
that evening at the house, in a line that

00:57:51.260 --> 00:57:55.570
also serves as another callback to that Dewey
and Gale scene from Scream 2. This time instead

00:57:55.570 --> 00:58:00.099
of Dewey quoting a specific page from Gale’s
book, he’s quoting Sidney’s book in regards

00:58:00.099 --> 00:58:01.339
to his marriage.

00:58:01.339 --> 00:58:07.300
–[Dewey] Just when you think things can’t get
any worse, sometimes they don’t. Sometimes

00:58:07.300 --> 00:58:13.020
they get better. Out of Darkness by Sidney
Prescott, page 220.

00:58:13.020 --> 00:58:16.360
– Later in the evening, after Ghostface
comes for Olivia, Sidney tries to get into

00:58:16.360 --> 00:58:19.910
The Morris Household to save her, and she
does so by throwing a potted plant through

00:58:19.910 --> 00:58:20.910
the window.

00:58:20.910 --> 00:58:24.340
Sidney has always been seen as the slasher
successor to Laurie Strode from Halloween,

00:58:24.340 --> 00:58:27.293
with her story even running parallel to Laurie’s
in Scream 1, and now we’ve got her throwing

00:58:27.293 --> 00:58:30.390
a potted plant at a house, like Laurie in
Halloween.

00:58:30.390 --> 00:58:34.110
As always, the movie is packed with Halloween
references. When Sidney makes a guest appearance

00:58:34.110 --> 00:58:38.070
at the Cinema Club, Robbie hypes up her presence
by comparing her to the actresses who starred

00:58:38.070 --> 00:58:39.289
in Halloween and The Exorcist.

00:58:39.289 --> 00:58:44.089
– [Robbie] Beyond Jamie Lee Curtis, forget
Linda Blair, this is the ultimate.

00:58:44.089 --> 00:58:48.039
– Which is also its own kind of joke because
they both appear in Scream 1. The cinema club

00:58:48.039 --> 00:58:52.069
holds their party, Stab-a-thon, at an abandoned
farm house, much like the party in Halloween 5.

00:58:52.069 --> 00:58:56.369
But if you look closer, there’s a for
sale sign outside the farm that says Roberts

00:58:56.369 --> 00:59:00.100
Reality. Roberts refers to Sidney’s mom’s
side of the family. Her mother was Maureen

00:59:00.100 --> 00:59:04.349
Roberts before getting married, and Sidney’s
aunt is Kate Roberts. She is the realtor that

00:59:04.349 --> 00:59:08.020
this sign is referring to. Earlier drafts
of the script make mention of her profession.

00:59:08.020 --> 00:59:11.289
Kate takes Sidney in while she’s back in
Woodsboro. We can assume that Sidney’s dad

00:59:11.289 --> 00:59:14.980
moved away or died, because he’s not in
the movie. So Sidney is staying with a realtor,

00:59:14.980 --> 00:59:17.869
who happens to be selling the location of
“Stab-a-thon”.

00:59:17.869 --> 00:59:21.220
And this is another similarity she has to
Laurie Strode, who, after losing her parents

00:59:21.220 --> 00:59:25.240
is taken in by the Strode family, and her
new adoptive father is a realtor who is selling

00:59:25.240 --> 00:59:29.150
a house, the Myers House, which was the location
of more of a literal Stab-a-thon.

00:59:29.150 --> 00:59:32.550
With Sidney being part of the older generation
now, she takes responsibility for the kids’

00:59:32.550 --> 00:59:36.030
safety. Towards the end, she instructs Jill
to hide under a bed and then makes it look

00:59:36.030 --> 00:59:39.849
as if she’s helped her escape off the balcony.
This is very similar to Laurie’s strategy

00:59:39.849 --> 00:59:40.849
in Halloween.

00:59:40.849 --> 00:59:44.180
Of course, there’s one line where Sid is
made out to look more less like Laurie and

00:59:44.180 --> 00:59:45.540
more like her brother…

00:59:45.540 --> 00:59:50.499
–[Jill] You just won’t die will you? Who are
you? Michael f*cking Myers?

00:59:50.499 --> 00:59:53.750
– The last Halloween reference I want to
bring up is this poster seen in the classroom

00:59:53.750 --> 00:59:57.869
where Cinema Club is held. If you’ve seen
Rob Zombie’s Halloween 2, you know it is

00:59:57.869 --> 01:00:02.599
not a remake of Halloween 2. Rob Zombie’s
Halloween 1 is a remake of the original Halloween,

01:00:02.599 --> 01:00:07.320
however Rob Zombie’s Halloween 2 goes off
in it’s own… strange direction.

01:00:07.320 --> 01:00:10.690
I’m guessing this was likely a hint about
what the plans were for the Scream franchise.

01:00:10.690 --> 01:00:15.059
In January 2010, screenwriter Kevin Williamson,
who had returned after an absence in Scream 3

01:00:15.059 --> 01:00:19.690
stated that he was contracted to make Scream
4 and Scream 5. However, the fourth movie

01:00:19.690 --> 01:00:23.560
underperformed at the box office, director
Wes Craven got brain cancer, and the fifth

01:00:23.560 --> 01:00:27.079
installment had to be put on the shelf. Save
that one for a rainy decade.

01:00:27.079 --> 01:00:30.650
But as long as we’re talking about the meta
of Scre4m, I’ve also gotta mention what

01:00:30.650 --> 01:00:36.329
is perhaps the most meta thing in the whole
franchise.

01:00:36.329 --> 01:00:43.329
♪ Suspenseful Music ♪

01:00:43.329 --> 01:00:47.569
Remember that cop character, Anthony Perkins?
Well not only is he named after a Psycho actor,

01:00:47.569 --> 01:00:52.040
but he also appeared in Scary Movie 3. Scary
Movie is a parody of Scream and Scream is

01:00:52.040 --> 01:00:53.270
a satire of the horror genre.

01:00:53.270 --> 01:00:56.540
If you’ve kept up with my Scream Things
You Missed series, you already know that the

01:00:56.540 --> 01:01:00.450
first Scream contained many references to
slasher movies, including Wes Craven’s own

01:01:00.450 --> 01:01:04.760
work. So Scream 4 focuses on movies that were
remade. The new version of the video store

01:01:04.760 --> 01:01:08.609
scene is the Cinema Club scene where we see
a whole bunch of movie posters, like this

01:01:08.609 --> 01:01:12.771
one: The Hills Have Eyes. This checks both
boxes, it was directed by Wes Craven and it

01:01:12.771 --> 01:01:14.400
received a remake in 2006.

01:01:14.400 --> 01:01:17.940
If you’re really paying attention you’ll
also notice the poster for The People Under

01:01:17.940 --> 01:01:18.940
The Stairs,

01:01:18.940 --> 01:01:21.609
a 1991 Wes Craven production. Before his death,

01:01:21.609 --> 01:01:24.270
Craven planned to remake this, but never quite
got around to it.

01:01:24.270 --> 01:01:28.330
There’s also a poster for George Romero’s
Dawn of the Dead. It was remade in 2004. The

01:01:28.330 --> 01:01:32.780
Thing is a remake of The Thing From Another
World from 1951. You may notice the poster

01:01:32.780 --> 01:01:36.710
on the left is not a horror movie, it’s
a film called Zack and Miri Make a Porno.

01:01:36.710 --> 01:01:40.039
This is actually a continuation of a series
of easter eggs between Wes Craven and Kevin

01:01:40.039 --> 01:01:43.970
Smith. I talked about this in greater detail
in my Scream 3 episode, but the two directors

01:01:43.970 --> 01:01:47.500
have been exchanging cameos and easter eggs
in their movies since the original Scream.

01:01:47.500 --> 01:01:50.619
If you look around the classroom, you’ll
also see that the Cinema Club are big fans

01:01:50.619 --> 01:01:54.690
of the Stab franchise. You can see the Stab
poster right here, and the Stab 3 poster back

01:01:54.690 --> 01:01:59.039
in the corner. An interesting note here is
the tagline for Stab 3 is “Hollywood Horror”.

01:01:59.039 --> 01:02:03.080
Which means this is not the same movie we
saw being shot in Scream 3, whose tagline

01:02:03.080 --> 01:02:06.789
was “Return To Woodsboro.” It seems that
since all the cast was killed, the studio

01:02:06.789 --> 01:02:11.529
decided to make Stab 3 about that, rather
than trying to recast for Return To Woodsboro.

01:02:11.529 --> 01:02:14.980
Little did they know, the story would return
to Woodsboro all on its own.

01:02:14.980 --> 01:02:18.790
Behind this shelf is the Stab 5 poster, and
the Stab 6 poster. But that’s really only

01:02:18.790 --> 01:02:23.430
just the beginning of the display of their
Stab fandom. We see much more that night at

01:02:23.430 --> 01:02:26.240
Stab-a-thon where there are a lot of background
details you may have missed.

01:02:26.240 --> 01:02:29.810
Here’s a girl dressed as Casey Becker, posing
with a decoration of the “gutted” Casey

01:02:29.810 --> 01:02:33.619
Becker, which you can see more of behind the
crowd right here. Another display shows Tatum

01:02:33.619 --> 01:02:37.680
Riley hanging out of the garage flap. Many
of the attendees are wearing costumes of Scream

01:02:37.680 --> 01:02:41.750
and Stab characters. Something that is very
accurate if you’ve ever been to a screening.

01:02:41.750 --> 01:02:45.490
These two guys in the audience are both dressed
as Dewey, and one of them even has a mustache

01:02:45.490 --> 01:02:49.319
hat. There’s another Casey Becker on the
left. A lot of the kids have Woodsboro High

01:02:49.319 --> 01:02:53.021
letterman jackets, like Casey’s boyfriend
Steve Orth, but then again, those are also

01:02:53.021 --> 01:02:56.839
their actual high school jackets, so I won’t
count that one. But I will count this couple

01:02:56.839 --> 01:03:01.039
dressed as Gale and Dewey, Gale in her green
news dress and Dewey in his uniform.

01:03:01.039 --> 01:03:03.289
– [Gale] This party is all about the Stab
movies, come on! How meta can you get?

01:03:03.289 --> 01:03:08.119
– [Dewey] How what-a can you get?
– [Gale] I don’t know, I heard him say it.

01:03:09.119 --> 01:03:12.230
– Gale places cameras around the party much
like she did in Scream, so that she can watch

01:03:12.230 --> 01:03:15.839
from her vehicle. In fact, one of the cameras
is right beneath the movie screen, just as

01:03:15.839 --> 01:03:18.809
the surveillance camera in Scream was placed
under Stu’s television.

01:03:18.809 --> 01:03:22.119
Now you’re probably wondering: are there
differences between the version of Stab we

01:03:22.119 --> 01:03:26.980
saw in Scream 2 and what we see in Scream
4? So I cut both films apart and lined them

01:03:26.980 --> 01:03:31.270
both up, and the answer is: yes there are
a lot of differences. Enough to make a whole

01:03:31.270 --> 01:03:35.320
video about it, which I plan on doing. But
for this video, I’m only gonna talk about

01:03:35.320 --> 01:03:36.320
the ones that are interesting.

01:03:36.320 --> 01:03:40.880
In Scream 4 we see this card at the beginning:
A Robert Rodriguez Film. This sort-of qualifies

01:03:40.880 --> 01:03:44.470
as a crew reference easter egg, because Robert
Rodriguez was brought in to film the Stab

01:03:44.470 --> 01:03:45.780
footage for Scream 2.

01:03:45.780 --> 01:03:49.190
After lining up all the cut points, there’s
an interesting synchronization that occurs.

01:03:49.190 --> 01:03:53.579
Which is that Gale gets stabbed at the same
time as Casey in Stab, which in Scream 2,

01:03:53.579 --> 01:03:57.039
occurred at the same time Maureen was stabbed
inside the movie theater. Just thought that

01:03:57.039 --> 01:03:58.040
was kind of cool.

01:03:58.040 --> 01:04:01.760
Before being attacked, Gale holds her camera
by her waist, looking behind her, unintentionally

01:04:01.760 --> 01:04:05.369
recreating the moment from Scream where the
characters in the news van see Ghostface sneaking

01:04:05.369 --> 01:04:06.419
up behind Randy.

01:04:06.419 --> 01:04:07.979
–[Kenny] Behind you kid!
–[Sidney] Randy!

01:04:07.979 --> 01:04:10.170
– This time it’s Dewey, concerned about
Gale.

01:04:10.170 --> 01:04:12.410
–[Dewey] Gale! Gale behind you!

01:04:12.410 --> 01:04:16.089
– This is more than just a nostalgic callback
though, it also sets up a scene later where

01:04:16.089 --> 01:04:20.230
Robbie puts his headset on backwards. We’re
expecting Ghostface to appear behind him,

01:04:20.230 --> 01:04:23.710
so it’s more of a surprise when the killer
ends up being right in front of him, just

01:04:23.710 --> 01:04:26.869
another example of this movie’s mission
to do the unexpected.

01:04:26.869 --> 01:04:30.210
That also includes the film’s ending. We
are led to believe that it will end at the

01:04:30.210 --> 01:04:32.400
party, just like the original, but…

01:04:32.400 --> 01:04:35.109
–[Charlie] This is what the reboots do, they one-up
the original ending.

01:04:35.109 --> 01:04:38.380
–[Kirby] The Woodsboro Murders ended at a party.
So in the remake…

01:04:38.380 --> 01:04:40.840
–[Charlie] The party’s the false ending. New rules.

01:04:40.840 --> 01:04:44.240
– However, this also ends up being a false
ending, the true ending takes place at the

01:04:44.240 --> 01:04:48.670
hospital. Just as there were two false openings,
there are also two false endings. This is

01:04:48.670 --> 01:04:51.670
also something that happens in the Halloween
remake. The original ends at the Doyle house,

01:04:51.670 --> 01:04:55.180
but the remake continues on and ends at the
Myers house. There are probably other examples

01:04:55.180 --> 01:04:57.349
of remakes in the 2000s doing this though.

01:04:57.349 --> 01:05:01.790
So in this case, we have the quote unquote
remake continuing on past the original. Whereas,

01:05:01.790 --> 01:05:05.980
in the 90s, if you wanted to continue past
the original, you had to watch Scream 2, which

01:05:05.980 --> 01:05:09.290
contains a scene of a character named Cici
watching the movie Nosferatu, and getting

01:05:09.290 --> 01:05:12.880
killed shortly after. For this reason, I find
it fitting that Kirby’s room displays a

01:05:12.880 --> 01:05:16.690
Nosferatu poster, but who knows if there was
any actual meaning behind this choice, it’s

01:05:16.690 --> 01:05:18.900
kind of a stretch on my part to make that
connection.

01:05:18.900 --> 01:05:22.680
However, I am convinced that the wardrobe
choices were definitely done for a reason.

01:05:22.680 --> 01:05:26.160
I talked before about how each character in
Scre4m represents one of the characters from

01:05:26.160 --> 01:05:30.910
Scream. However, after the reveal of the killers,
all of those roles are reassigned. Jill is

01:05:30.910 --> 01:05:35.260
now Billy, the mastermind behind the murders.
Charlie is still Stu, the accomplice hoping

01:05:35.260 --> 01:05:40.000
to vent his romantic frustrations with a knife.
Trevor is now Neil Prescott, the tied up victim

01:05:40.000 --> 01:05:41.000
who is going to be framed.

01:05:41.000 --> 01:05:44.910
So it’s no mistake that Jill is wearing
a blue plaid shirt, just like what Billy wore

01:05:44.910 --> 01:05:50.210
at the party, and Trevor is wearing this exact
outfit that Neil was captured in back in 1996.

01:05:50.210 --> 01:05:53.839
Other callbacks include Sidney escaping off
the roof, Charlie being tied to a chair like

01:05:53.839 --> 01:05:58.039
Steve Orth, Kirby being quizzed on horror
movies like Casey Becker, and of course, this

01:05:58.039 --> 01:05:59.420
iconic line.

01:05:59.420 --> 01:06:03.440
–[Jill] Hello Sidney. Surprised?
–[Mickey] Surprise, Sidney.

01:06:03.440 --> 01:06:05.050
–[Stu] Surprise, Sidney.

01:06:05.050 --> 01:06:09.371
– I imagine she’s getting tired of hearing
that. In my personal opinion, Scre4m was one

01:06:09.371 --> 01:06:13.200
of the more satisfying surprises though, which
makes all the clues about the killers’ identities

01:06:13.200 --> 01:06:16.760
all the more impressive.

01:06:16.760 --> 01:06:21.790
♪ Upbeat Music ♪

01:06:21.790 --> 01:06:27.829
Upon rewatching the movie, I noticed that
while Jill and Charlie pretend they aren’t

01:06:27.829 --> 01:06:31.530
particularly close, they do sit next to each
other in class, and Jill is the only one not

01:06:31.530 --> 01:06:34.660
to take out her phone when everyone is getting
alerts about Jenny and Marnie, though I’m

01:06:34.660 --> 01:06:37.369
not sure why she doesn’t at least pretend
to be interested.

01:06:37.369 --> 01:06:41.539
On a somewhat related note, fellow Zac and
fellow horror creator Zack Cherry pointed

01:06:41.539 --> 01:06:45.260
out in his video that Sidney doesn’t appear
to have a cell phone in this movie. She receives

01:06:45.260 --> 01:06:49.500
calls on Jill’s cell and on the Roberts’
landline, and after arriving at Kirby’s

01:06:49.500 --> 01:06:52.990
she borrows Jill’s phone to place an outgoing
call to Dewey to plea for help.

01:06:52.990 --> 01:06:55.910
This is probably because Sidney is tired of
all the prank calls and doesn’t want to

01:06:55.910 --> 01:07:00.430
give any future copycat killers a means of
contacting her, it’s a nice touch. Mr. Cherry

01:07:00.430 --> 01:07:04.470
also attributes a lot of the kills to Charlie,
which many were surprised by, but I personally

01:07:04.470 --> 01:07:07.809
think it kind of makes sense. Jill would have
wanted to set it up that way because of the

01:07:07.809 --> 01:07:10.900
fact that they’re recording all of the kills
and the fact that she’s secretly planning

01:07:10.900 --> 01:07:13.849
to frame Charlie as the one of the killers
alongside Trevor.

01:07:13.849 --> 01:07:17.150
It stands to reason that the police would
probably study these videos, and take note

01:07:17.150 --> 01:07:21.310
of the killer’s height and broad shoulders.
Charlie might also be the most forceful Ghostface

01:07:21.310 --> 01:07:23.740
we’ve seen. Check this out.

01:07:23.740 --> 01:07:24.740
(woman wimpering)

01:07:24.740 --> 01:07:28.270
When I analyzed Scream 1, I mentioned that
the poster in Stu’s room matched a poster

01:07:28.270 --> 01:07:32.010
displayed in the video store, and that this
linked his character to the movie obsessed

01:07:32.010 --> 01:07:36.849
killer. A similar tactic is used here. Jill’s
room is filled with clues. This Farewell Republic

01:07:36.849 --> 01:07:41.640
poster and this… uhh, I don’t know, reverse
Griffon?... are both posters seen hanging

01:07:41.640 --> 01:07:42.640
in the Cinema Club.

01:07:42.640 --> 01:07:46.151
I will mention the possibility that there
is actually no meaning behind that. They could

01:07:46.151 --> 01:07:50.230
have just reused posters and hoped nobody
would notice. Because this War of The Worlds

01:07:50.230 --> 01:07:53.990
poster in Jill’s room also shows up in her
English class. I can’t think of any reason

01:07:53.990 --> 01:07:57.470
they’d want to link Jill to her English
classroom. So it’s very possible that the

01:07:57.470 --> 01:08:00.680
art department just ran out of posters and
I’m just looking for things that aren’t

01:08:00.680 --> 01:08:01.680
there.

01:08:01.680 --> 01:08:04.940
But at the very least, we can conclude that
Jill is more interested in horror movies and

01:08:04.940 --> 01:08:09.040
serial killers than she likes to let on, from
her American Werewolf in London poster, to

01:08:09.040 --> 01:08:14.090
her Edgar Allan Poe-ster, to her Gale Weathers
books, books on psychology and serial killers

01:08:14.090 --> 01:08:18.529
and even her Fall Out Boy poster; this particular
one is the cover for their single I Don’t

01:08:18.529 --> 01:08:22.710
Care, whose chorus matches her MO for the
movie. “I. Don’t. Care what you think.

01:08:22.710 --> 01:08:24.230
As long as it’s about me.”

01:08:24.230 --> 01:08:27.890
This might be more happenstance, but this
is also off their album Folie a Deux, which

01:08:27.890 --> 01:08:31.549
is French for madness in pairs, a term that
could probably be used to describe each pair

01:08:31.549 --> 01:08:35.920
of Ghostface killers. I love this album, and
I’d love to talk more about it, but this

01:08:35.920 --> 01:08:39.310
is not a music analysis channel, so let’s
get back to Scre4m.

01:08:39.310 --> 01:08:42.710
After Olivia is killed, Charlie comes out
of the dark to attack Sidney and Jill, and

01:08:42.710 --> 01:08:46.770
Jill receives a cut on her forearm, the same
spot Derek got cut in Scream 2.

01:08:46.770 --> 01:08:52.450
–[Medic] No struggle? He just cut you and ran away?
You’re lucky he didn’t kill you.

01:08:52.450 --> 01:08:54.020
–[Dewey] Yeah, it’s awfully convenient.

01:08:54.020 --> 01:08:55.020
–[Derek] Say what?

01:08:55.020 --> 01:08:56.020
–[Dewey] Nothing,

01:08:56.020 --> 01:08:57.449
it’s just a shame he got away so easily.

01:08:57.449 --> 01:08:59.320
–[Derek] It’s just a shame you got there too
late!

01:08:59.320 --> 01:09:03.029
– Of course, Derek ended up not being guilty,
so Dewey would probably want to avoid making

01:09:03.029 --> 01:09:05.159
any similar accusation about Jill.

01:09:05.159 --> 01:09:08.640
When Sidney speaks to Jill the following night,
she has what she thinks is a bonding moment

01:09:08.640 --> 01:09:09.640
with her cousin.

01:09:09.640 --> 01:09:13.390
– [Sidney] You know when people say, ‘I
know how you feel’, but they’re really

01:09:13.390 --> 01:09:19.749
just saying that because they have no freaking
idea how you feel? I know how you feel.

01:09:19.749 --> 01:09:23.429
– I love that line, because in reality,
Sidney has no idea how Jill feels because

01:09:23.429 --> 01:09:26.859
she doesn’t suspect her of being the killer.
It’s like the sticker on the door of the

01:09:26.859 --> 01:09:29.530
Cinema Club says: Not your usual suspects.

01:09:29.530 --> 01:09:33.029
Jill claims she could never handle the attention
of being the surviving victim, when in fact,

01:09:33.029 --> 01:09:38.200
that’s exactly what she wants, even if her
15 minutes of fame really is only 15 minutes.

01:09:38.200 --> 01:09:42.170
And finally, at Kirby’s afterparty, Trevor
shows up unexpectedly, and just walks right

01:09:42.170 --> 01:09:43.600
in because the door is unlocked.

01:09:43.600 --> 01:09:48.540
– [Trevor] By the way Kirby, with everything
that’s going on right now, you probably shouldn’t leave that unlocked.

01:09:48.540 --> 01:09:49.759
– [Charlie] Sorry.

01:09:49.759 --> 01:09:53.330
Charlie is not worried about locking the door
because he knows there’s no threat. He and

01:09:53.330 --> 01:09:55.150
Jill are both in the room already.

01:09:55.150 --> 01:09:59.380
Scre4m would be the final legacy of director Wes Craven, but his creation would live on

01:09:59.380 --> 01:10:03.658
without him after yet another 11 year gap
with Scream (2022). 

01:10:03.658 --> 01:10:07.840
Most Scream fans already know about how Scream
(2022) paid tribute to Wes Craven, the director

01:10:07.840 --> 01:10:11.960
of Screams 1-4, by naming a character after
him, having a house party dedicated to that

01:10:11.960 --> 01:10:15.370
character after his death, and repeating the
words: “For Wes”, as the first thing we

01:10:15.370 --> 01:10:18.980
see in the end credits. What you might not
have noticed was the hidden meaning behind

01:10:18.980 --> 01:10:23.199
Wes’s phone number. The “707” is just
the area code for the part of Northern California

01:10:23.199 --> 01:10:27.239
where Woodsboro would be located, and all
the Woodsboro characters have this area code,

01:10:27.239 --> 01:10:30.590
but “1939” refers to the year Wes Craven
was born.

01:10:30.590 --> 01:10:34.100
We actually see Craven’s birthday come up
again later during the YouTube video about

01:10:34.100 --> 01:10:41.820
Stab (2021). The channel’s subscriber count
is 821,939, which could be read as 8/2/1939.

01:10:41.820 --> 01:10:46.449
Under the American date format of month, day,
year, this represents August 2, 1939, the

01:10:46.449 --> 01:10:47.860
day Wes Craven was born.

01:10:47.860 --> 01:10:52.199
This page contains a lot of other easter eggs,
including other hidden dates in the numbers.

01:10:52.199 --> 01:10:56.660
To learn about many classic cameos, secrets
on tattoos and meanings behind names on cell

01:10:56.660 --> 01:10:59.890
phones, stick around to the end of this video.

01:10:59.890 --> 01:11:02.290
♪ Metal Music ♪

01:11:02.290 --> 01:11:10.050
Welcome to Things You Missed. When I heard
there was going to be a new Scream movie,

01:11:10.050 --> 01:11:13.570
I was hoping that the franchise would come
back to tackle the phenomenon of requels,

01:11:13.570 --> 01:11:18.490
like Halloween (2018), Jurassic World and
Candyman (2021). Then, I started watching

01:11:18.490 --> 01:11:24.659
Scream (2022) and realized, oh yeah, Scre4m
already was a requel. Also, I generally don’t

01:11:24.659 --> 01:11:25.680
really like requels.

01:11:25.680 --> 01:11:29.120
But what I do like about them though, is that
they’re basically easter nests. What do

01:11:29.120 --> 01:11:33.980
I mean by that? A nest is somewhere you might
find a lot of eggs. In this case easter eggs,

01:11:33.980 --> 01:11:37.909
as in the hidden secrets found in contemporary
media. One of my favorite things to analyze

01:11:37.909 --> 01:11:41.440
on Things You Missed. So let’s get in to
the Things You Missed.

01:11:41.440 --> 01:11:44.100
♪ Mysterious Music ♪

01:11:44.100 --> 01:11:50.230
Each entry in the Scream franchise serves
as a satire of the horror genre and sometimes

01:11:50.230 --> 01:11:54.030
of media franchises as a whole. As you are
very aware of if you’ve seen my previous

01:11:54.030 --> 01:11:59.110
videos on Scream, Scream 2, Scream 3 and Scre4m,
there are a few franchises that they like

01:11:59.110 --> 01:12:03.480
to use as a blueprint of sorts. First there’s
Halloween, the movie shown in the first Scream,

01:12:03.480 --> 01:12:07.060
which served as an example of horror movie
rules. Then there’s Star Wars, which was

01:12:07.060 --> 01:12:10.540
most heavily discussed in Scream 3 when the
characters were trying to figure out the rules

01:12:10.540 --> 01:12:14.870
for trilogies. Finally there’s Scream, which
also exists in-universe where it’s known

01:12:14.870 --> 01:12:16.540
as the Stab franchise.

01:12:16.540 --> 01:12:20.960
So many things that happen in Scream (2022),
from bigger plot points to little moments,

01:12:20.960 --> 01:12:24.219
are callbacks to things that have happened
in the Scream franchise, which is also fitting

01:12:24.219 --> 01:12:28.220
for a requel, because these movies often share
the same concept of: “it’s happening again.”

01:12:28.220 --> 01:12:32.550
So for the third time in franchise history,
it starts with a phone ringing, then a female

01:12:32.550 --> 01:12:36.100
picking it up and speaking to an anonymous
caller, then she starts playing with a knife

01:12:36.100 --> 01:12:39.610
block, then she gets attacked by Ghostface,
and then she kicks her attacker in an attempt

01:12:39.610 --> 01:12:40.690
to escape.

01:12:40.690 --> 01:12:44.199
Then in a parallel to Scream 1, a group of
friends discusses the attack the next day

01:12:44.199 --> 01:12:48.530
while sitting outside of Woodsboro High School
while eating grapes. We find out boyfriend

01:12:48.530 --> 01:12:52.640
and girlfriend Chad and Liv aren’t sleeping
together, like Sidney and Billy in Scream;

01:12:52.640 --> 01:12:56.659
an announcement from the high school principal
cancels classes, just like in Scream. But

01:12:56.659 --> 01:12:59.420
did you notice who the new principal was?

01:12:59.420 --> 01:13:03.710
– [Principal] Attention, Panthers. A reminder
that all

01:13:03.710 --> 01:13:05.710
classes and school activities are canceled

01:13:05.710 --> 01:13:07.700
tomorrow due to public safety concerns.

01:13:07.700 --> 01:13:10.270
– According to executive producer
Chad Villela,

01:13:10.270 --> 01:13:11.980
in this interview with Bloody Disgusting,

01:13:11.980 --> 01:13:15.641
that was a voice cameo from Drew Barrymore,
who is known for playing Casey Becker in the

01:13:15.641 --> 01:13:19.510
original Scream. There are many other voice
cameos present in the movie. Most of them

01:13:19.510 --> 01:13:23.010
can be heard during Amber’s party. The first
comes from Matthew Lillard, who played Stu

01:13:23.010 --> 01:13:24.010
in Scream.

01:13:24.010 --> 01:13:25.730
–[Party Guy] Hey Freeman, cool house!

01:13:25.730 --> 01:13:27.840
– Fitting, considering that this is
Stu’s

01:13:27.840 --> 01:13:30.230
old house. Several cameo voices overlap when

01:13:30.230 --> 01:13:32.909
the guests make a toast to the character Wes.

01:13:32.909 --> 01:13:36.710
– [Chad] Hey. To Wes!
– [crowd] To Wes!

01:13:36.710 --> 01:13:38.370
– The article states that many people who
knew

01:13:38.370 --> 01:13:41.050
Wes Craven contributed voices for this line,

01:13:41.050 --> 01:13:44.850
including 3 Scream actors: Henry Winkler,
who played Principal Himbry, Adam Brody, who

01:13:44.850 --> 01:13:48.630
played Officer Hoss in Scre4m and Hayden Panettiere,
who played Kirby.

01:13:48.630 --> 01:13:52.489
Co-director Tyler Gillett added that Jamie
Kennedy, who played Randy in the original

01:13:52.489 --> 01:13:54.520
Scream trilogy, contributed this line:

01:13:54.520 --> 01:13:57.352
–[Jamie] Somebody’s goofy ass dad is kicking
us out.

01:13:57.352 --> 01:13:58.352
– …which he actually says right while
his

01:13:58.352 --> 01:14:00.510
character is delivering his lines in Stab,

01:14:00.510 --> 01:14:04.949
which is playing on the TV, where we can see
another cameo. Co-director Matt Bettinelli-Olpin

01:14:04.949 --> 01:14:09.179
revealed that he plays the on-screen Michael
Myers in the movie-within-the-movie-within-the-movie.

01:14:09.179 --> 01:14:12.790
Maybe we should stop calling it Scream and
start calling it Shrek because this thing

01:14:12.790 --> 01:14:17.199
has got layers. Speaking of Halloween, there’s
also a reference to its director.

01:14:17.199 --> 01:14:18.860
– [Sam] My name is Samantha Carpenter.

01:14:18.860 --> 01:14:21.570
– This is an homage to Halloween director
John

01:14:21.570 --> 01:14:23.010
Carpenter. Or possibly Wes Carpenter.

01:14:23.010 --> 01:14:26.900
– [Tatum] Don't go there Sid, you're starting
to sound like some Wes Carpenter flick or

01:14:26.900 --> 01:14:27.900
something.

01:14:27.900 --> 01:14:29.449
– The new directors for Scream (2022) went
a

01:14:29.449 --> 01:14:31.630
little crazy with references to Wes Craven’s

01:14:31.630 --> 01:14:33.081
body of work, starting with Sidney Prescott’s
address.

01:14:33.081 --> 01:14:36.810
– [Tara] Sidney Prescott! It’s Sidney
Prescott and she lived on Elm.

01:14:36.810 --> 01:14:39.730
Although it was mentioned in the original
script, I don’t think that Sid’s street

01:14:39.730 --> 01:14:43.679
address was ever brought up in the first movie.
But Elm is a reference to Elm Street, from

01:14:43.679 --> 01:14:47.429
Wes Craven’s famous franchise, A Nightmare
on Elm Street. We actually see the Elm Street

01:14:47.429 --> 01:14:50.110
intersection later when Sam and Richie roll
into town.

01:14:50.110 --> 01:14:53.590
Then, when we’re reintroduced to Dewey,
you can see the list of his cell phone contacts

01:14:53.590 --> 01:14:56.949
if you pause at the right moment. One of his
contacts is “Kristy” which is probably

01:14:56.949 --> 01:15:02.010
a reference to Kristy Parker from A Nightmare
on Elm St 3 and 4. Kristy is played by Patricia

01:15:02.010 --> 01:15:05.580
Arquette, the real life sister of Dewey's
actor: David Arquette.

01:15:05.580 --> 01:15:09.790
Also of note in Dewey’s list of contacts
is B Miska. Which is a nod to Brad Miska,

01:15:09.790 --> 01:15:13.610
the owner of horror movie news website Bloody
Disgusting, which may explain why they have

01:15:13.610 --> 01:15:17.630
all these exclusive interviews with the directors
and producers, which I keep referencing. During

01:15:17.630 --> 01:15:21.790
the YouTube scene later on, the related videos
panel also suggests an upload from the Bloody

01:15:21.790 --> 01:15:25.680
Disgusting channel; another exclusive interview,
but we’ll get to that later.

01:15:25.680 --> 01:15:29.909
Under B Miska, keeping up with the theme of
internet horror personalities is Mr. Morris.

01:15:29.909 --> 01:15:34.250
That’s me, Zac Morris. You might be thinking,
no it’s just the father of Olivia Morris

01:15:34.250 --> 01:15:37.539
from Scre4m. But no. She doesn’t have a
father.

01:15:37.539 --> 01:15:40.409
– [Kirby] Your mom home?
– [Olivia] She’s at her boyfriend’s.

01:15:40.409 --> 01:15:42.511
– So it must be talking about me. If Dead
Meat

01:15:42.511 --> 01:15:45.010
gets to be in it, I get to be in it too, damnit!

01:15:45.010 --> 01:15:48.500
Another one of Dewey’s contacts is Judy,
which is probably just Judy Hicks, his old

01:15:48.500 --> 01:15:51.940
deputy at the police station. I was thinking
it’s kind of a shame that he never got with

01:15:51.940 --> 01:15:56.460
her after he split up with Gale, but who knows,
maybe he did. Actually, now that I think about

01:15:56.460 --> 01:16:00.360
it, she must have already had a kid by the
time she was hitting on Dewey in Scre4m so…

01:16:00.360 --> 01:16:01.360
that’s weird.

01:16:01.360 --> 01:16:05.800
We actually see her list of contacts too,
and you can spot more Wes Craven related homages.

01:16:05.800 --> 01:16:10.010
Tina G represents Tina Gray from the original
Nightmare on Elm Street. Leroy is a character

01:16:10.010 --> 01:16:15.320
from Craven’s 1991 film, The People Under
The Stairs. Nancy T, of course, is Nancy Thompson,

01:16:15.320 --> 01:16:19.280
the main girl from A Nightmare on Elm Street.
B Adams is Brandon Adams, the actor who played

01:16:19.280 --> 01:16:21.090
Fool in The People Under The Stairs.

01:16:21.090 --> 01:16:25.469
Under her son, there are three deputies listed.
Deputy Vinson and Deputy Farney are actual

01:16:25.469 --> 01:16:29.840
characters in the movie. Deputy Keith is not,
I’m guessing this is a crew reference easter

01:16:29.840 --> 01:16:32.719
egg, because the movie’s production designer
is named Chad Keith.

01:16:32.719 --> 01:16:36.030
For all the Wes Craven related easter eggs,
it seems like there’s only one easter egg

01:16:36.030 --> 01:16:40.630
related to Kevin Williamson, the screenwriter
of Screams 1, 2 and 4. It comes when Tara

01:16:40.630 --> 01:16:44.730
is in the hospital watching an episode of
Dawson’s Creek. This is Season 1 Episode

01:16:44.730 --> 01:16:48.780
11: The Scare, which was written by Williamson,
and the episode itself is kind of spoof of

01:16:48.780 --> 01:16:52.370
the original Scream. Like I said: there are
a lot of layers to this.

01:16:52.370 --> 01:16:56.260
We’re not done with the Wes Craven easter
eggs though. I’ll be continuing to analyze

01:16:56.260 --> 01:16:59.909
those, and Richie’s big slip up, that would
have given him away as the killer if only

01:16:59.909 --> 01:17:03.020
his girlfriend had noticed.

01:17:03.020 --> 01:17:05.040
(impact)

01:17:05.040 --> 01:17:11.090
♪ Dreadful Music ♪

01:17:11.090 --> 01:17:15.040
Throughout the movie, we see a couple of memorials
to past characters. Dewey has a box dedicated

01:17:15.040 --> 01:17:19.290
to his sister, Tatum; and Martha has a home
theater dedicated to her brother, Randy. On

01:17:19.290 --> 01:17:22.950
the mantle there’s also a photo of a movie
theater marquee with three movies playing:

01:17:22.950 --> 01:17:26.989
A Nightmare on Elm Street, The Hills Have
Eyes and Last House on the Left -- all Wes

01:17:26.989 --> 01:17:28.310
Craven directed films.

01:17:28.310 --> 01:17:31.909
Sam Carpenter is essentially the new Sidney
Prescott in this movie. Much like Sidney’s

01:17:31.909 --> 01:17:35.610
father in the original, we learn Sam’s mom
is out of town. Both of their mothers are

01:17:35.610 --> 01:17:39.910
cheaters, and both characters have some psychological
issues caused by Billy Loomis -- Sidney because

01:17:39.910 --> 01:17:44.100
Billy killed her mother, and Sam because Billy
is her real father. Throughout the series

01:17:44.100 --> 01:17:48.320
I’ve discussed how Sidney shares similarities
with Halloween’s protagonist Laurie Strode.

01:17:48.320 --> 01:17:51.989
Sam takes after her as well. Laurie was a
babysitter in the original Halloween, and

01:17:51.989 --> 01:17:55.050
it turns out Sam has some experience in childcare
as well.

01:17:55.050 --> 01:17:57.750
– [Sam] There are Chad and Mindy, the twins.
And

01:17:57.750 --> 01:17:59.610
Wes. I used to babysit them all.

01:17:59.610 --> 01:18:01.480
– The first victim to die this time around
is

01:18:01.480 --> 01:18:03.909
Vince, who we eventually learn to be Stu Macher’s

01:18:03.909 --> 01:18:07.980
nephew. In a requel, everyone has to have
some dumb reason to be related to legacy characters.

01:18:07.980 --> 01:18:11.060
There’s actually a little clue about this
in the opening scene, where Tara looks up

01:18:11.060 --> 01:18:14.770
the cast of the original Stab on her phone.
There’s a new addition here, a cast member

01:18:14.770 --> 01:18:19.200
who wasn’t specified back in Scream 2, and
that is Vince Vaughn playing the role of Stu

01:18:19.200 --> 01:18:22.130
Macher. So the names Vince and Stu are kind
of linked from the beginning.

01:18:22.130 --> 01:18:26.011
There’s also a detail on the hood of Vince’s
car. Not only a movie detail, but like an

01:18:26.011 --> 01:18:29.711
actual car detail. The words: Six Pack are
written there, perhaps a hint that we’ll

01:18:29.711 --> 01:18:33.630
be seeing a sixth movie. Also this guy parked
in front of him is parked in front of a fire

01:18:33.630 --> 01:18:36.040
hydrant. Come on man, the cops are right there.

01:18:36.040 --> 01:18:40.160
Vince also has a Jason tattoo. This is probably
a red herring to make the audience think he

01:18:40.160 --> 01:18:42.820
might be the killer, considering the line
Richie had earlier.

01:18:42.820 --> 01:18:47.449
–[Richie] And I gotta make sure we don’t get sliced
up by some lunatic who saw Friday the 13th

01:18:47.449 --> 01:18:51.959
and thought, ‘you know what? That Jason
guy, he’s got some pretty solid ideas.’

01:18:51.959 --> 01:18:54.139
– Of course, Vince ends up being a
victim, not

01:18:54.139 --> 01:18:55.850
a killer. During his death scene, we hear

01:18:55.850 --> 01:19:00.150
the song Red Right Hand, which appeared in
every Scream movie besides Scre4m, so it’s

01:19:00.150 --> 01:19:04.530
good to see it back on rotation here. Ghostface
actually takes out Vince with his own switchblade,

01:19:04.530 --> 01:19:08.030
not the usual Ghostface knife. I’m guessing
this is just another red herring though, to

01:19:08.030 --> 01:19:10.540
make it look like Vince was somehow connected.

01:19:10.540 --> 01:19:14.780
Finally after like 35 minutes, we see Sidney
Prescott. She’s a runner now, which seems

01:19:14.780 --> 01:19:18.480
like a reasonable hobby after a lifetime of
being chased by psychopaths, and she finally

01:19:18.480 --> 01:19:22.489
owns a cell phone. She never had one in Scre4m,
but seeing as how that didn’t prevent her

01:19:22.489 --> 01:19:26.360
from being targeted by the next Ghostface,
she probably decided to just cave in and get

01:19:26.360 --> 01:19:27.360
one.

01:19:27.360 --> 01:19:30.020
Dewey mentions that she’s married to someone
named Mark. There is a Mark in the Scream

01:19:30.020 --> 01:19:34.580
franchise, that is, Detective Mark Kincaid,
the cop from Scream 3 who weirdly came back

01:19:34.580 --> 01:19:38.770
to her house and watched movies with the trio
at the end. One of the Bloody Disgusting articles

01:19:38.770 --> 01:19:42.910
I mentioned earlier addresses this; the executive
producer confirms that Sidney’s husband

01:19:42.910 --> 01:19:47.030
is indeed Mark Kincaid. I can’t say this
fully makes sense to me, because where was

01:19:47.030 --> 01:19:51.520
he in Scre4m and where is he now? And why
is Sidney’s name still Sidney Prescott?

01:19:51.520 --> 01:19:55.580
I’m guessing she still wanted to take advantage
of the notoriety of her name for her career

01:19:55.580 --> 01:19:56.580
as an author.

01:19:56.580 --> 01:20:00.230
The original Scream featured a scene known
as the video store scene, where Randy speculates

01:20:00.230 --> 01:20:04.520
on who the killer might be. Every subsequent
sequel so far has had its own version of the

01:20:04.520 --> 01:20:05.520
video

01:20:05.520 --> 01:20:09.170
store scene. This time, it’s the Randy Meeks
Memorial Theater scene, and Mindy, the new

01:20:09.170 --> 01:20:12.280
Randy, fills the gang in on her ideas about
who might be guilty.

01:20:12.280 --> 01:20:16.380
At one point during this scene, Dewey refers
to Stu Macher as “A real Looney Tune.”

01:20:16.380 --> 01:20:20.340
This may be a reference to the fact that Stu
was in the 2003 movie: Looney Tunes: Back

01:20:20.340 --> 01:20:24.739
in Action, where he plays himself, Matthew
Lillard arguing, with Shaggy (also voiced

01:20:24.739 --> 01:20:28.640
by Lillard) about the quality of his performance
in Scooby-Doo: The Movie; a scene probably

01:20:28.640 --> 01:20:31.750
meta enough to be in a Scream movie, if we’re
being honest.

01:20:31.750 --> 01:20:35.300
In the background, there’s a Vertigo poster.
This is now the third Scream movie in a row

01:20:35.300 --> 01:20:40.020
to reference Vertigo, starting with Sarah’s
line in Scream 3, and this poster in Scre4m.

01:20:40.020 --> 01:20:43.860
Vertigo is directed by Alfred Hitchcock, who
borderline invented the slasher genre with

01:20:43.860 --> 01:20:47.840
his movie: Psycho. Psycho has been referenced
in every Scream movie, and that continues

01:20:47.840 --> 01:20:52.340
here, with this poster, Amber imitating the
Psycho soundtrack as she gestures her knife…

01:20:52.340 --> 01:20:53.349
–[Amber] Ee, ee, ee!

01:20:53.349 --> 01:20:56.590
– …One of the Ghostface killers even directly
cites the movie this time around.

01:20:56.590 --> 01:20:58.590
– [Killer] Ever seen the movie Psycho?

01:20:58.590 --> 01:21:01.340
– And then we cut directly to this shot,
where

01:21:01.340 --> 01:21:03.179
Wes is taking a shower. It’s the same camera

01:21:03.179 --> 01:21:07.260
angle used in the shower scene from Psycho
and subsequently, the shower scene from Stab

01:21:07.260 --> 01:21:08.489
in the second Scream.

01:21:08.489 --> 01:21:12.949
The next character to die is Wes. As I mentioned,
he gets out of the shower and explores around

01:21:12.949 --> 01:21:16.480
downstairs. In the kitchen there’s a note
from his mom. “Wes, Lemon squares in the

01:21:16.480 --> 01:21:17.630
fridge. ♥ Mom”.

01:21:17.630 --> 01:21:21.710
This is a callback to Judy’s famous lemon
squares, which she made for Dewey in Scre4m.

01:21:21.710 --> 01:21:24.990
– [Judy] Sheriff, you’re not cheatin’
on your wife if

01:21:24.990 --> 01:21:26.080
you eat my lemon square.

01:21:26.080 --> 01:21:27.690
– The ones that Gale found disgusting.

01:21:27.690 --> 01:21:30.660
– [Gale] Your lemon squares taste like ***.

01:21:30.660 --> 01:21:32.580
Speaking
of whom, she returns to the story by stepping

01:21:32.580 --> 01:21:37.389
out of this KQIS 6 news van. It’s the same
station that Gale worked for in Scream, but

01:21:37.389 --> 01:21:42.320
ever since then, she’s worked other jobs
such as uptodate, 60 Minutes II, Total Entertainment,

01:21:42.320 --> 01:21:46.090
and she even went rogue for a while. Seeing
her back at her original station is exactly

01:21:46.090 --> 01:21:49.910
the choice you’d expect from a requel looking
not to upset the status quo.

01:21:49.910 --> 01:21:54.110
At this point, Dewey and Gale are divorced,
an event we already saw brewing in Scre4m,

01:21:54.110 --> 01:21:57.739
but it sounds like their divorce went exactly
like their break-up in between Scream 2 and

01:21:57.739 --> 01:22:01.770
Scream 3: Gale gets an important job opportunity
in a big city and the two can’t make it

01:22:01.770 --> 01:22:06.580
work long distance. Dewey belongs in Woodsboro,
Gale belongs in a big city reporter job. Once

01:22:06.580 --> 01:22:09.700
again, history seems to be repeating itself
with these two.

01:22:09.700 --> 01:22:13.590
Despite Gale being back in her old job, the
underlying theme about the media and its effect

01:22:13.590 --> 01:22:15.840
on real world violence is not as present in
the

01:22:15.840 --> 01:22:17.909
new Scream like it was in the first four movies.

01:22:17.909 --> 01:22:21.420
We do get a little bit of discourse about
the entertainment news industry at the end,

01:22:21.420 --> 01:22:24.900
where Richie seems to imply he was driven
to the brink partly by the media blaming the

01:22:24.900 --> 01:22:28.840
fans for the poor reception of the new Stab
movie, but I see that as just one aspect of

01:22:28.840 --> 01:22:31.920
a bigger conversation around this idea of
toxic fandom.

01:22:31.920 --> 01:22:36.761
So what is toxic fandom? Is it real? Is it
a problem? Should we be worried about it?

01:22:36.761 --> 01:22:40.800
The most agreed upon definition reads: When
a person believes they have the only correct

01:22:40.800 --> 01:22:44.739
way of appreciating certain content. They
will attack other fans that disagree with

01:22:44.739 --> 01:22:48.989
them even if they are discussing a topic the
toxic fan knows little about or has only heard

01:22:48.989 --> 01:22:50.120
from second-hand sources.

01:22:50.120 --> 01:22:54.740
I will add that it seems that “toxic fandom”
has also become a buzzword used by journalists

01:22:54.740 --> 01:22:58.560
or salty creators to try to get clicks and
sway a narrative. For example, let’s say

01:22:58.560 --> 01:23:02.830
a new movie from a popular franchise comes
out and it’s bad. Those involved might soon

01:23:02.830 --> 01:23:06.969
be crying about “toxic fandom” to try
to make all criticisms look invalid. I’m

01:23:06.969 --> 01:23:10.449
sure there are fans in those cases who dislike
that new movie merely because it does not

01:23:10.449 --> 01:23:13.969
fit their belief about what that franchise
should be like, but that does not make the

01:23:13.969 --> 01:23:18.389
entire fandom toxic, and it shouldn’t invalidate
good criticisms based on the quality of the

01:23:18.389 --> 01:23:19.389
work.

01:23:19.389 --> 01:23:23.110
So is it a problem, should we be worried about
it? At the end of the day, it’s all online

01:23:23.110 --> 01:23:26.340
discourse. There’s no reason to take it
that seriously. I feel like I can offer a

01:23:26.340 --> 01:23:30.650
unique perspective here as someone who is
both a fan and a creator. As a fan, I try

01:23:30.650 --> 01:23:34.530
to separate my bias from my critique. For
example, I don’t really like the concept

01:23:34.530 --> 01:23:38.330
of Toy Story 4, because I think the third
was such a perfect ending. But I don’t think

01:23:38.330 --> 01:23:42.150
it’s a bad movie. I recognize that it’s
well made for what it is. But when something

01:23:42.150 --> 01:23:45.920
is bad, I think it’s important to be vocal
about it in a constructive way. As a fan,

01:23:45.920 --> 01:23:49.600
it shows that you care, and a good creator
will take quality criticism into account for

01:23:49.600 --> 01:23:50.600
future work.

01:23:50.600 --> 01:23:54.460
As a creator, you have to be able to separate
good criticism from comments by those who

01:23:54.460 --> 01:23:58.020
were never going to like your content in the
first place. And that brings us to my favorite

01:23:58.020 --> 01:24:02.380
cameo in the movie, the Dead Meat cameo, and
this webpage has more hidden content than

01:24:02.380 --> 01:24:05.730
you could possibly imagine.

01:24:05.730 --> 01:24:08.260
(impact)

01:24:08.260 --> 01:24:13.310
♪ Intense Music ♪

01:24:13.310 --> 01:24:17.060
For those unaware, the couple behind the “Film
Fails” channel, which Richie can be seen

01:24:17.060 --> 01:24:20.850
watching on his laptop, is actually James
A Janisse and his wife, Chelsea -- the hosts

01:24:20.850 --> 01:24:24.780
of YouTube biggest horror movie channel, Dead
Meat, who we can actually see a real upload

01:24:24.780 --> 01:24:26.880
from in the recommended bar on the right.

01:24:26.880 --> 01:24:30.190
It struck me as strange that they would use
a made-up brand for their channel, because

01:24:30.190 --> 01:24:33.820
this is a Scream movie. All of the movies
and shows referenced tend to be real movies

01:24:33.820 --> 01:24:38.570
and shows from our world, the only exception
being Stab, which is a stand-in for a real

01:24:38.570 --> 01:24:39.890
franchise: the Scream franchise.

01:24:39.890 --> 01:24:43.300
I figured I might as well go straight to the
source on this one, because I can, so I asked

01:24:43.300 --> 01:24:46.860
James why that might be. This is what he had
to say: “Mostly cause we're being a-holes

01:24:46.860 --> 01:24:50.680
in it. It's a parody of more toxic YouTube
channels that cover movies, it's not meant

01:24:50.680 --> 01:24:54.180
to be us or Dead Meat.” I think this is
a great answer, because it fits right in with

01:24:54.180 --> 01:24:58.170
what I was saying earlier about toxic fandom.
These Film Fails characters aren’t saying

01:24:58.170 --> 01:25:01.699
anything constructive about the movie, they’re
basically just pouting that it’s not the

01:25:01.699 --> 01:25:05.850
Stab that they know and are comfortable with.
And when I think more about it, there is precedent

01:25:05.850 --> 01:25:10.210
for this. Sunrise Studios doesn’t exist,
it was just a parody of a predatory movie

01:25:10.210 --> 01:25:14.520
studio. KQIS 6 doesn’t exist either, it’s
a parody of bloodthirsty journalism.

01:25:14.520 --> 01:25:18.420
Film Fails, more specifically, seems to parody
YouTube channels that screeched about Star

01:25:18.420 --> 01:25:22.970
Wars: The Last Jedi in 2017, judging by that
Dead Meat upload on the side: “Rian Johnson

01:25:22.970 --> 01:25:26.830
on Reinventing Stab”. Rian Johnson was the
director of that very heavily debated Star

01:25:26.830 --> 01:25:30.929
Wars title, and was often the target of that
vitriol from fans and content creators. I’m

01:25:30.929 --> 01:25:34.570
sure there are many people that would like
you to believe that The Last Jedi was divisive

01:25:34.570 --> 01:25:38.489
or controversial, but if you actually look
at the ratings, most people found it to be

01:25:38.489 --> 01:25:39.489
very average.

01:25:39.489 --> 01:25:42.949
I already talked about Film Fails’s subscriber
count being Wes Craven’s birthday, but the

01:25:42.949 --> 01:25:50.800
view count is also significant. It has 122,096
views; 12/20/96. December 20th, 1996 was the

01:25:50.800 --> 01:25:54.980
release date of the original Scream. Then
there’s the like to dislike ratio. It has

01:25:54.980 --> 01:26:01.949
1,117 likes and 20 dislikes; 11/17/20 or November
17th, 2020 was the day that production wrapped

01:26:01.949 --> 01:26:06.720
on Scream 2022. Thanks to Richie for using
the Return YouTube Dislike extension so we

01:26:06.720 --> 01:26:09.100
could see that one.
On the side, there’s a video from Bloody

01:26:09.100 --> 01:26:12.449
Disgusting’s YouTube channel that I mentioned
earlier. It’s titled: “Interview with

01:26:12.449 --> 01:26:16.610
Woodsboro Survivor Kirby Reed!” Kirby was
a major character in the fourth movie, and

01:26:16.610 --> 01:26:19.770
up until now, we were lead to believe that
she didn’t make it, so this is actually

01:26:19.770 --> 01:26:20.770
big news!

01:26:20.770 --> 01:26:25.390
The fourth video is called “Not My STAB!
Join the StabHead Army and #RedoStab2021!!”

01:26:25.390 --> 01:26:30.480
and it’s uploaded by a channel called StabHead.
There’s a bit of a story behind this, so

01:26:30.480 --> 01:26:34.340
grab some popcorn. Dreadit is the name of
the horror movie community on Reddit. Mindy

01:26:34.340 --> 01:26:37.659
actually mentions it when she’s explaining
the fan contempt for Stab 8.

01:26:37.659 --> 01:26:41.610
–[Mindy] You go on 4chan and Dreadit, all they’re
talking about is how Stab 8 pissed on their

01:26:41.610 --> 01:26:42.610
childhoods.

01:26:42.610 --> 01:26:44.070
– To promote Scream (2022)’s release,
the

01:26:44.070 --> 01:26:45.929
team behind the movie held an ask-me-anything

01:26:45.929 --> 01:26:48.870
session on Dreadit. One user, named StabHead
asked, “Did

01:26:48.870 --> 01:26:51.270
any of the actors go above and beyond to prepare

01:26:51.270 --> 01:26:55.180
for their roles?” Their reply said: “We
were meeting with Jack to talk about his character

01:26:55.180 --> 01:26:59.770
and he told us that he had created a Reddit
account under the name StabHead to… ...wait

01:26:59.770 --> 01:27:02.699
a minute… WE LOVE YOU JACK QUAID!!!”

01:27:02.699 --> 01:27:06.870
Necessary information: Jack Quaid is the actor
who played Richie, the second Ghostface killer.

01:27:06.870 --> 01:27:10.920
The account shows him commenting on topics
like Halloween, The Last Jedi, and of course,

01:27:10.920 --> 01:27:11.920
Scream.

01:27:11.920 --> 01:27:15.630
So the way I see it, StabHead is actually
Richie’s online persona. He was the one

01:27:15.630 --> 01:27:19.350
to create this channel and upload this video.
Amber even mentions during the finale that

01:27:19.350 --> 01:27:23.469
she met Richie on the Stab subreddit. Under
the StabHead upload, there’s one more video

01:27:23.469 --> 01:27:28.100
of importance. “Did the real-life Stu Macher
survive?” As I mentioned in my Scream 3

01:27:28.100 --> 01:27:32.050
video, the original plan for that movie was
to have Stu return as the villain, but since

01:27:32.050 --> 01:27:35.840
this was right after Columbine, producers
decided it was not the best idea to have a

01:27:35.840 --> 01:27:39.989
high school revenge seeker right now. But
this video title suggests that maybe having

01:27:39.989 --> 01:27:44.070
Stu return in the future is not yet out of
the question. If he is alive, he’s been

01:27:44.070 --> 01:27:48.409
in prison for about 25 years now. Although
Stu himself isn’t in the movie, his actor

01:27:48.409 --> 01:27:52.459
has yet another vocal cameo here as the voice
of the new silver-faced Ghostface.

01:27:52.459 --> 01:27:54.730
–[Matthew] This sh*t is lit!

01:27:54.730 --> 01:27:56.080
– One of the common complaints about the
new

01:27:56.080 --> 01:27:58.739
Ghostface in Stab (2021) is his new weapon:

01:27:58.739 --> 01:28:02.600
a flamethrower. Ironically, the new Ghostface
in this movie ends up getting herself

01:28:02.600 --> 01:28:08.050
lit on fire. The link to Film Fails’s merch
store is hastaelfuego.org -- Spanish for until

01:28:08.050 --> 01:28:11.880
the fire, which could also be a clue about
Amber’s fate. When she has her one last

01:28:11.880 --> 01:28:15.550
scare moment at the end, she’s covered in
burn scars, very reminiscent of Freddy Krueger.

01:28:15.550 --> 01:28:18.100
You can bet that that was by design.

01:28:18.100 --> 01:28:21.949
About mid-way through the film, the deaths
of Judy and Wes are discovered, and Sam realizes

01:28:21.949 --> 01:28:26.020
her sister may be in trouble at the hospital,
so she gives Richie a call to check on her,

01:28:26.020 --> 01:28:28.539
and hears an unexpected voice on the other
line.

01:28:28.539 --> 01:28:37.159
–[caller] Hello Samantha. Richie can’t come to
the phone right now. He’s finding out what

01:28:37.159 --> 01:28:41.030
happens to people who stick their noses in
business that doesn’t concern them.

01:28:41.030 --> 01:28:44.920
– It reminds me of Jigsaw re-recording Daniel
Matthews voicemail greeting in Saw 2.

01:28:44.920 --> 01:28:46.630
– [Jigsaw] You’ve reached Daniel's phone.
He is

01:28:46.630 --> 01:28:47.630
not in right now.

01:28:47.630 --> 01:28:52.010
– I love a good horror movie voicemail greeting.
If this was the fourth movie, which had a

01:28:52.010 --> 01:28:56.091
lot of Saw references, this might be an intentional
nod to Saw, but this is the fifth movie, which

01:28:56.091 --> 01:29:00.429
focuses more on the so-called “elevated
horror” genre. For example, Tara’s favorite

01:29:00.429 --> 01:29:04.540
movie is the The Babadook and she begs to
be quizzed on the gems of the mid-to-late

01:29:04.540 --> 01:29:05.540
10s.

01:29:05.540 --> 01:29:09.230
– [Tara] Ask me about It Follows. Ask me
about Hereditary. Ask me about The VVitch.

01:29:09.230 --> 01:29:13.590
– She’s got really good taste! She describes
elevated horror as being “scary but with

01:29:13.590 --> 01:29:18.000
complex emotional and thematic underpinnings”,
not just cheesy jump scares, which Richie,

01:29:18.000 --> 01:29:19.789
as a fan of Stab, isn’t thrilled with.

01:29:19.789 --> 01:29:21.729
– [caller] Sounds kind of boring to me.

01:29:21.729 --> 01:29:24.139
– And in the Home Theater scene, Stab’s
slasher

01:29:24.139 --> 01:29:27.090
stylings and “elevated horror” are painted

01:29:27.090 --> 01:29:30.060
to be opposites. A major reason cited for
fans not liking the newest Stab movie was

01:29:30.060 --> 01:29:33.790
the “crammed in social commentary”. So
Richie was upset that his favorite franchise

01:29:33.790 --> 01:29:37.770
was trying to ride the trendy wave of elevated
horror, and set out to craft his own entry

01:29:37.770 --> 01:29:42.250
more in line with the original by doing a
requel. The huge gaping question that leaves

01:29:42.250 --> 01:29:47.330
is: isn’t that exactly what Jill already
tried to do? And yes, it is, the only difference

01:29:47.330 --> 01:29:51.730
was their motivations. Jill did it for fame,
but Richie and Amber just wanted to motivate

01:29:51.730 --> 01:29:55.139
the creation of another Stab movie -- it
doesn’t seem like being the star was as

01:29:55.139 --> 01:29:56.139
important to them.

01:29:56.139 --> 01:29:59.510
Even if we are to give them the benefit of
the doubt and say that these killers are an

01:29:59.510 --> 01:30:03.780
homage to Scre4m, rather than copying it,
there are still a couple of details necessary

01:30:03.780 --> 01:30:07.340
to make it make sense, which I didn’t fully
realize on my first viewing. The first is

01:30:07.340 --> 01:30:11.801
that there is no Stab movie based on Scre4m.
If there was, Richie and Amber probably wouldn’t

01:30:11.801 --> 01:30:16.030
have felt the need to make a new one. At the
time of Scre4m there are 7 Stab movies, and

01:30:16.030 --> 01:30:18.960
in this movie, there’s only one new one,
hence the #RedoStab8.

01:30:18.960 --> 01:30:23.949
Then, there’s the fact that the original
Scream, and really all the Scream movies do

01:30:23.949 --> 01:30:27.909
feature some kind of social commentary -- which
I’ve taken time to discuss in each of my

01:30:27.909 --> 01:30:29.719
Things You Missed™ videos. So why would
Richie

01:30:29.719 --> 01:30:31.590
be upset about the new Stab having social

01:30:31.590 --> 01:30:36.449
commentary? This is because in the Scream
universe, the Stab movies are considered non-fiction.

01:30:36.449 --> 01:30:39.840
Nobody is making up the plot, so nothing that
happens qualifies as commentary.

01:30:39.840 --> 01:30:44.979
–[Richie] Because that’s how you make a great
Stab movie, Sam. “Based on actual events”.

01:30:44.979 --> 01:30:46.450
– And this ties right back into the theme
of

01:30:46.450 --> 01:30:48.791
toxic fandom: Richie believes each Stab movie

01:30:48.791 --> 01:30:52.710
must be like the original. That’s why there
are so many repeated elements from the original

01:30:52.710 --> 01:30:54.950
Scream. Like when Tara is trying to defend
herself at the hospital.

01:30:54.950 --> 01:30:55.950
(Tara grunts)

01:30:55.950 --> 01:30:56.950
–[Richie] Ow! Ahh! God dammnit.

01:30:56.950 --> 01:30:59.080
Did you hit me with a phone?

01:30:59.080 --> 01:31:01.670
–[Tara] LOOKOUT!

01:31:01.670 --> 01:31:03.110
– Must have been thrilling for Richie to
be

01:31:03.110 --> 01:31:04.880
reliving events from the original Stab.

01:31:04.880 --> 01:31:07.810
– [Sidney] You’ve gotta find me first
you pansy-***

01:31:07.810 --> 01:31:08.810
momma’s boy!

01:31:08.810 --> 01:31:11.830
– [Billy] ****
– [Stu] You ****ing hit me with a phone,

01:31:11.830 --> 01:31:12.830
****.

01:31:12.830 --> 01:31:14.030
– In some cases lines are nearly lifted
directly

01:31:14.030 --> 01:31:15.249
from a previous movie.

01:31:15.249 --> 01:31:17.179
– [caller] You think you're still the star.

01:31:17.179 --> 01:31:19.560
– [Sidney] This isn't a f*cking movie!
– [caller] It will be.

01:31:19.560 --> 01:31:24.940
– [Sam] This isn’t a f*cking movie.
– [Richie] No, but it will be…

01:31:24.940 --> 01:31:28.770
– The final act features plenty more references,
some of which you might have noticed, but

01:31:28.770 --> 01:31:33.429
you probably missed this tiny detail, which
was the reason Mindy was able to survive where

01:31:33.429 --> 01:31:35.510
others did not.

01:31:35.510 --> 01:31:41.139
–[Amber] Welcome to Act 3.

01:31:41.139 --> 01:31:42.550
–[Richie] RUN!

01:31:42.550 --> 01:31:43.960
(impact)

01:31:43.960 --> 01:31:46.769
♪ Suspenseful Music ♪

01:31:46.769 --> 01:31:48.350
– Before we get to the final act, let’s
talk

01:31:48.350 --> 01:31:50.560
about Dewey. His fatal mistake is shooting

01:31:50.560 --> 01:31:54.560
Amber’s bulletproof armor rather than hitting
her in the head. He realizes it a little too

01:31:54.560 --> 01:31:58.420
late. In Scream 3 he makes the same mistake
with Roman, but Sidney is there to remind

01:31:58.420 --> 01:32:01.940
him to aim for the head. This time she isn’t,
and Dewey pays the price.

01:32:01.940 --> 01:32:06.060
Given Dewey’s ability to survive seemingly
fatal blows in the past, I wasn’t convinced

01:32:06.060 --> 01:32:10.389
that he was really done for this time. We’ve
seen this scene before. However, the more

01:32:10.389 --> 01:32:14.840
I look at it, the more I think he really might
be gone this time. He’s being taken to the

01:32:14.840 --> 01:32:19.449
coroner this time, not the ambulance. I doubt
the coroner would be called unless some medical

01:32:19.449 --> 01:32:21.790
professional was really sure that he was dead.

01:32:21.790 --> 01:32:26.230
– [Amber] Dewey had to die to make it real.
To show that this wasn’t just some bullsh*t,

01:32:26.230 --> 01:32:29.050
cash-in, run of the mill sequel.

01:32:29.050 --> 01:32:30.150
– Well, this is awkward.

01:32:30.150 --> 01:32:34.480
It also fits the blueprint of a requel. When
the cast is talking about requels, Mindy brings

01:32:34.480 --> 01:32:38.480
up Star Wars: The Force Awakens as an example.
There will be spoilers for Star Wars: The

01:32:38.480 --> 01:32:42.170
Force Awakens here, so if you don’t want
to hear them, skip forward to the next chapter.

01:32:42.170 --> 01:32:46.369
The Force Awakens is all about passing the
torch to a new set of characters. Luke, Leia

01:32:46.369 --> 01:32:50.710
and Han are passing it off to Rey, Fin and
Poe. This is accentuated by the death of a

01:32:50.710 --> 01:32:55.820
legacy character: Han Solo. So Scream (2022)
is doing the same here by killing off Dewey

01:32:55.820 --> 01:33:00.280
as the torch is passed from Sidney, Dewey
and Gale to Sam, Tara, Mindy and Chad.

01:33:00.280 --> 01:33:04.389
And just as The Force Awakens brought back
familiar locations like The Millenium Falcon

01:33:04.389 --> 01:33:08.739
and The Starkiller, which is basically The
Death Star, Scream (2022) brings back the

01:33:08.739 --> 01:33:12.449
Macher household, which plays host to another
party, which is probably the most tasteless

01:33:12.449 --> 01:33:16.030
thing ever, considering it’s supposedly
supposed to honor their friend Wes, but it

01:33:16.030 --> 01:33:19.639
looks like a typical college party. I guess
it’s kind of fitting though, considering

01:33:19.639 --> 01:33:21.340
the existence of this movie.

01:33:21.340 --> 01:33:24.889
Here we see a few more callbacks. The basement
fridge is the new garage fridge.

01:33:24.889 --> 01:33:27.829
–[Robert] Not many people have basements in California.

01:33:27.829 --> 01:33:29.540
– Richie is wearing a blue plaid shirt,
just

01:33:29.540 --> 01:33:31.270
like former Woodsboro killers Billy Loomis

01:33:31.270 --> 01:33:35.030
and Jill Roberts. When Sidney arrives, the
first door she checks behind is the same one

01:33:35.030 --> 01:33:39.070
she hid inside when she donned the Ghostface
mask in the original Scream. Perhaps the most

01:33:39.070 --> 01:33:42.580
obvious repeat is the infamous, “look behind
you” scene, which we’ve seen play out

01:33:42.580 --> 01:33:46.969
in various forms across most Scream movies.
Mindy is the new Randy, so fittingly, she’s

01:33:46.969 --> 01:33:50.900
the one being snuck up on, but unlike Randy,
she realizes it and attempts to get out of

01:33:50.900 --> 01:33:54.449
the way. This is partially because she knows
she’s in a movie and she might be a little

01:33:54.449 --> 01:33:58.080
bit sharper than Randy, but there’s also
an important clue in Stab.

01:33:58.080 --> 01:34:02.050
There are a couple of VHS tapes on the table
behind Stab Randy. I think the top one might

01:34:02.050 --> 01:34:05.670
be The People Under Stairs, another Wes Craven
reference, and I’m having a hard time making

01:34:05.670 --> 01:34:09.380
out what the bottom one is, let me know if
you recognize it. But look directly below

01:34:09.380 --> 01:34:13.340
the TV she’s watching on, and you’ll see
the same three VHS tapes, two of which are

01:34:13.340 --> 01:34:17.179
the same two on the table behind Randy. There’s
a slightly better look a bit later, when Sam

01:34:17.179 --> 01:34:20.600
is tending to Mindy’s injuries, it’s definitely
the same two tapes.

01:34:20.600 --> 01:34:24.699
Mindy, by the way, does survive, which could
be related to this little line from Scre4m.

01:34:24.699 --> 01:34:27.219
– [Charlie] In fact, the only surefire way
to survive

01:34:27.219 --> 01:34:29.650
a modern horror movie, you pretty much have

01:34:29.650 --> 01:34:30.650
to be gay.

01:34:30.650 --> 01:34:32.580
– [Zac] It also helps to be the daughter
of a serial

01:34:32.580 --> 01:34:34.330
killer, as it turns out. She may be related

01:34:34.330 --> 01:34:38.150
to Billy Loomis, but Sam wipes off her knife
a lot more like Stu Macher.

01:34:38.150 --> 01:34:41.600
Now before I get into the ending, and my views
on what this really means for the franchise,

01:34:41.600 --> 01:34:45.710
I’ve got to go over all the evidence seen
throughout the film that proves who the killers

01:34:45.710 --> 01:34:49.811
really were, as I have at the end of each
of my Scream analysis videos. So let’s take

01:34:49.811 --> 01:34:51.489
it back one more time to the start.

01:34:51.489 --> 01:34:55.219
In the opening, Tara invites Amber to come
over, mentioning that her Mom was out of town.

01:34:55.219 --> 01:34:58.850
Amber and Richie were probably waiting for
an opportunity like this and used it to decide

01:34:58.850 --> 01:35:00.860
that this would be the night they strike.

01:35:00.860 --> 01:35:04.400
When Tara starts receiving concerning phone
calls, she gets a text from Amber, which says

01:35:04.400 --> 01:35:08.130
“you should answer it.” The caller explains
that Amber’s phone was cloned and no one

01:35:08.130 --> 01:35:13.080
really questions it. But the easier and correct
explanation is that Amber was just the killer,

01:35:13.080 --> 01:35:16.690
using her own phone. When Sam gets a Ghostface
call at the hospital, it’s also from

01:35:16.690 --> 01:35:17.800
Amber’s phone.

01:35:17.800 --> 01:35:20.830
Amber and Richie have to act like they are
meeting for the first time. We see her look

01:35:20.830 --> 01:35:25.440
at him very lustfully, which makes more sense
now knowing that they are secretly a couple.

01:35:25.440 --> 01:35:28.821
Sam is attacked at the hospital at the same
time Amber was being questioned by Hicks at

01:35:28.821 --> 01:35:32.530
the police station, which means this one must
have been Richie. This is the same old trick

01:35:32.530 --> 01:35:37.051
used to get Billy Loomis off the hook in Scream,
get Roman off the hook in Scream 3. When are

01:35:37.051 --> 01:35:39.690
they gonna learn that there are always multiple
killers?

01:35:39.690 --> 01:35:43.280
Then there’s the scene where Dewey correctly
predicts a killer by accusing Richie. This

01:35:43.280 --> 01:35:47.330
is just like Randy in the original Scream,
his first guess is Billy, and it’s correct.

01:35:47.330 --> 01:35:49.710
–[Randy] He’s got killer printed all over his
forehead.

01:35:49.710 --> 01:35:51.770
– The most damning evidence comes after
he kicks

01:35:51.770 --> 01:35:53.530
Richie and Sam out though. Listen to what

01:35:53.530 --> 01:35:55.150
Richie has to say about him.

01:35:55.150 --> 01:36:00.340
–[Richie] Jesus! Yeah he’s way more fun in the
movies.

01:36:00.340 --> 01:36:04.260
– Notice the plural use of the word: movies.
At this point in the story, he claims that

01:36:04.260 --> 01:36:08.139
he’s only seen one Stab movie, the original.
When he and Sam were driving to Woodsboro

01:36:08.139 --> 01:36:11.840
he claimed to have no idea about the Stab
movies, and in the hospital, he says he’s

01:36:11.840 --> 01:36:16.040
watching the first one on Netflix so he can
be prepared. Lucky for him, Sam doesn’t

01:36:16.040 --> 01:36:17.360
seem to notice this slip up.

01:36:17.360 --> 01:36:21.000
While it’s not necessarily evidence, I do
think the fact that he spends his spare time

01:36:21.000 --> 01:36:25.740
watching vitriol about Stab (2021) kind of
telling about where his interests lie, even

01:36:25.740 --> 01:36:28.460
if his reasoning is that he’s trying to
learn about the franchise.

01:36:28.460 --> 01:36:31.900
Later on, during the second attack at the
hospital, Amber cuts Richie in the forearm.

01:36:31.900 --> 01:36:35.590
We saw this in Scre4m, where Jill was cut
in the same spot by her fellow accomplice

01:36:35.590 --> 01:36:39.460
as a way to deflect suspicion away from her.
In my previous video, I labeled this as a

01:36:39.460 --> 01:36:43.480
nod to Scream 2, where Derek is cut in the
forearm, and Dewey finds it suspicious because

01:36:43.480 --> 01:36:46.030
the cut conveniently missed major arteries.

01:36:46.030 --> 01:36:49.969
Despite some of the well hidden evidence I
mentioned, I found these killers to be pretty

01:36:49.969 --> 01:36:53.021
obvious. Perhaps the fact that they are so
similar to the killers from the last movie

01:36:53.021 --> 01:36:55.110
made them stand out a little bit more.

01:36:55.110 --> 01:36:58.690
We close with the same pull out shot of a
news anchor giving a report in front of the

01:36:58.690 --> 01:37:03.160
house, but this time it is not Gale Weathers.
Like the main characters and the filmmakers,

01:37:03.160 --> 01:37:06.639
the news anchor microphone has been passed
down to the next generation.

01:37:06.639 --> 01:37:11.949
At the end, over the dedication to the series’
late former director, we hear a bird call.

01:37:11.949 --> 01:37:14.230
(whip-poor-will call)

01:37:14.230 --> 01:37:17.800
It links back to all the bird artwork seen
in Amber’s house. It’s all part of one

01:37:17.800 --> 01:37:22.219
last tribute to Scream’s iconic director,
Wes Craven, who, in his spare time, was an

01:37:22.219 --> 01:37:23.330
avid birdwatcher.

01:37:23.330 --> 01:37:27.429
The sound at the end of Scream (2022) is the
call of the whip-poor-will, a sound effect

01:37:27.429 --> 01:37:31.699
that Craven has actually used a few times
in his films. In this 2008 interview in the

01:37:31.699 --> 01:37:36.770
bird enthusiast magazine Audubon, he explains
how he has used bird sounds as a sort of orchestration

01:37:36.770 --> 01:37:40.719
in his work. “I’ve used the whip-poor-will
a few times, probably inappropriately, but

01:37:40.719 --> 01:37:44.230
it’s such a haunting sound, and I do remember
the sound from being a kid in the woods of

01:37:44.230 --> 01:37:48.310
Ohio and on camping trips. But yeah, I think
they’re used a lot in Hollywood. I would

01:37:48.310 --> 01:37:51.760
be the first to confess that accuracy is probably
not the first motivation.”

01:37:51.760 --> 01:37:55.920
We get a quick flash of someone in a Ghostface
mask wiping off a knife before the credits

01:37:55.920 --> 01:37:59.159
roll, maybe an homage to the quick scares
seen before the credits of Scream

01:37:59.159 --> 01:38:00.949
(stinger)

01:38:00.949 --> 01:38:02.020
and in Scream 2

01:38:02.020 --> 01:38:03.900
(knives slashing and screaming)

01:38:03.900 --> 01:38:07.650
No such scare occurs in
Scream 3 or 4. To me, the implication is that

01:38:07.650 --> 01:38:10.000
seeing a new Ghostface right before the end
credits

01:38:10.000 --> 01:38:11.710
is teasing a sequel. We don’t see it in

01:38:11.710 --> 01:38:15.820
3 because Roman is defeated, and for now that
means the end of the series. While Scream

01:38:15.820 --> 01:38:20.090
4 was not planned to be the final movie, it
was the end of that era of Scream and there’s

01:38:20.090 --> 01:38:23.471
nothing in the story that prevents the fourth
movie from being the final chapter, unlike

01:38:23.471 --> 01:38:27.650
Scream 1, which specifically has a line about
how there’s gotta be a sequel, and Scream

01:38:27.650 --> 01:38:30.820
2, which specifically has a line suggesting
that it’s a trilogy.

01:38:30.820 --> 01:38:34.840
So the quick flash of Ghostface at the end
of Scream (2022), to me, is the directors’

01:38:34.840 --> 01:38:38.840
way of telling us they planned for a sequel,
and with a sixth movie being given the go-ahead

01:38:38.840 --> 01:38:42.960
before the fifth was even globally released,
it seems likely the next chapter was planned

01:38:42.960 --> 01:38:46.513
and will tie in nicely with the material in
the 2022 offering.

01:38:46.513 --> 01:38:50.000
(IMPACT)

01:38:54.874 --> 01:38:58.435
Towards the end of Scream VI, we see a quick
shot of the contacts list on

01:38:58.435 --> 01:39:00.510
Sam's phone, one of which is Kristy.

01:39:00.510 --> 01:39:03.310
In my Scream 2022 video, I pointed out the
Easter eggs hidden in the

01:39:03.310 --> 01:39:07.010
contacts on Dewey's phone and the meaning
behind Kristy and the other names listed.

01:39:07.010 --> 01:39:09.699
So this is our clue to be on the lookout for
references again.

01:39:09.699 --> 01:39:11.389
So let's go through her contacts.

01:39:11.389 --> 01:39:13.679
Tara is her sister, the Jenna Ortega character.

01:39:13.679 --> 01:39:16.800
But Meyer is the son of Scream VI producer
William Sherak.

01:39:16.800 --> 01:39:19.929
The next name is Eloise. She's the daughter
of William Sherak.

01:39:19.929 --> 01:39:21.490
The next name after that is Niky.

01:39:21.490 --> 01:39:24.969
The fifth and 6th Scream were directed by
Matt Bettinelli-Olpin and Tyler Gillett

01:39:24.969 --> 01:39:28.070
who did a segment in the anthology horror
film VHS where one of

01:39:28.070 --> 01:39:29.349
the characters is named Niky.

01:39:29.349 --> 01:39:32.679
I'm not sure about River, it's too common
of a word to search, but I did

01:39:32.679 --> 01:39:36.410
figure out that Eileen is Eileen Jones, the
wife of Tyler Gillett.

01:39:36.410 --> 01:39:38.290
Mindy and Chad are the twins from the movie.

01:39:38.290 --> 01:39:41.280
Sidney is the main protagonist of the franchise
who isn't in this one.

01:39:41.280 --> 01:39:42.550
Jay is the film's editor.

01:39:42.550 --> 01:39:45.179
Jay Prychidny, and T Ferg is the post production
assistant,

01:39:45.179 --> 01:39:46.199
Tyler Ferguson.

01:39:46.199 --> 01:39:49.969
I wasn't able to find a Minnie, and Rebecca
could refer to several people.

01:39:49.969 --> 01:39:53.739
But that's just a taste of the level of detail
that this movie's secrets have to offer.

01:39:53.739 --> 01:39:56.429
If you want to know the Scream origins of
every single prop in the

01:39:56.429 --> 01:39:59.360
movie theater shrine, all of the costume references
on the subway train and

01:39:59.360 --> 01:40:02.680
info hidden in hard to find places, stick
around to the end of this video.

01:40:03.351 --> 01:40:05.680
Welcome to Things You Missed.

01:40:05.680 --> 01:40:08.510
Just one year after the franchise was revived
with Scream 2022, the next

01:40:08.510 --> 01:40:11.261
sequel would already be released, mirroring
the quick turnaround from the

01:40:11.261 --> 01:40:14.860
original Scream in 1996 to Scream 2 in 1997.

01:40:14.860 --> 01:40:18.011
This would be our first of many clues that
Scream VI was going to be a

01:40:18.011 --> 01:40:21.820
requel of Scream 2 in the same way that the
last movie was a recall of the original.

01:40:21.820 --> 01:40:24.360
For the third movie in a row, they changed
the naming convention

01:40:24.360 --> 01:40:25.360
of the title.

01:40:25.360 --> 01:40:28.750
The fourth movie moved the number inside the
word Scream, which was cool.

01:40:28.750 --> 01:40:30.199
Everybody likes it when I call it Scre4m.

01:40:30.199 --> 01:40:33.179
It's not annoying, and nobody leaves angry
comments about that at all.

01:40:33.179 --> 01:40:35.949
The fifth could have gone with 5cream, but
instead it just decided to

01:40:35.949 --> 01:40:38.599
call it Scream to fit the meta of recalls
at the time,

01:40:38.599 --> 01:40:39.970
like Halloween and Candy Man.

01:40:39.970 --> 01:40:42.909
I guess Scream VI was trying to do the best
of both worlds by doing the

01:40:42.909 --> 01:40:46.330
standalone title, but also having the number
in the M on the poster,

01:40:46.330 --> 01:40:48.159
which means they had to switch to Roman numerals.

01:40:48.159 --> 01:40:50.320
It reminds me of the Jak franchise.

01:40:50.320 --> 01:40:52.969
The first game was Jak and Daxter: The Precursor
Legacy.

01:40:52.969 --> 01:40:54.650
Then Jak II switches to Roman numerals.

01:40:54.650 --> 01:40:57.800
Jack 3 used Arabic numerals, but then the
fourth one was called Jax X,

01:40:57.800 --> 01:41:00.360
but it wasn't the Roman numeral X, it was
just the letter X.

01:41:00.360 --> 01:41:02.449
Anyway, I'm getting off topic.

01:41:02.449 --> 01:41:06.179
Let's cut in to the Things You Missed.

01:41:06.179 --> 01:41:10.510
♪ Ominous Music ♪

01:41:10.510 --> 01:41:13.150
The first thing I'm going to talk about is
how similar this movie is to

01:41:13.150 --> 01:41:14.150
Scream 2.

01:41:14.150 --> 01:41:15.830
While the characters do point this out themselves

01:41:15.830 --> 01:41:19.530
-[Sam]Stab 2 took place in college.
-[Tara] So do we think that the killer is

01:41:19.530 --> 01:41:20.570
trying to copy the movies?

01:41:20.570 --> 01:41:22.880
It goes much deeper than you might have realized.

01:41:22.880 --> 01:41:25.500
In addition to the college setting, one of
the five Ghostface killers in

01:41:25.500 --> 01:41:28.530
this movie, Jason, is introduced in the opening
where we find out that he's

01:41:28.530 --> 01:41:31.320
a student in a class about 20th century slashers.

01:41:31.320 --> 01:41:34.430
Just like the killer in Scream 2, Mickey is
introduced in the college film

01:41:34.430 --> 01:41:36.219
class discussing the Stab movie.

01:41:36.219 --> 01:41:39.070
Both movies feature a main character who's
dealing with newfound fame after

01:41:39.070 --> 01:41:40.780
surviving murders the previous year.

01:41:40.780 --> 01:41:44.239
When a new string of crimes begins, they're
both given special police protection.

01:41:44.239 --> 01:41:47.429
Each has a new boyfriend who's very suspicious
but not guilty.

01:41:47.429 --> 01:41:50.210
When we're reintroduced to Tara, she's on
her way to a party

01:41:50.210 --> 01:41:52.449
-[Tara]She went to the Omega Kappa Beta party.

01:41:52.449 --> 01:41:55.410
It's the same fraternity that Derek belonged
to in Scream 2.

01:41:55.410 --> 01:41:58.219
We even briefly see their coat of arms that
matches the necklace that Derek

01:41:58.219 --> 01:41:59.219
gave Sidney.

01:41:59.219 --> 01:42:02.900
At the party, we learned that Tara is considering
joining sorority called Omega Beta Zeta.

01:42:02.900 --> 01:42:04.580
-[Frat Guy] You omega beta zeta.

01:42:04.580 --> 01:42:05.699
-[Tara] No, not yet.

01:42:05.699 --> 01:42:07.280
Which is the same one that CiCi Cooper

01:42:07.280 --> 01:42:09.330
was a part of in 1997.

01:42:09.330 --> 01:42:10.330
Speaking of CiCi Cooper,

01:42:10.330 --> 01:42:13.050
she doesn't last long in the movie, getting
stabbed and thrown off

01:42:13.050 --> 01:42:14.340
the top of that sorority house.

01:42:14.340 --> 01:42:18.030
She has a counterpart in Scream VI, Anika
who gets stabbed and tossed from

01:42:18.030 --> 01:42:19.530
the ladder she's trying to escape on.

01:42:19.530 --> 01:42:22.889
I don't know why Danny just has a huge ladder
in his NYC apartment.

01:42:22.889 --> 01:42:24.820
Maybe he saw Nerve and was inspired.

01:42:24.820 --> 01:42:28.119
Tara later explains that the Core Four moved
to New York for summer semester,

01:42:28.119 --> 01:42:31.139
very similar to how Sidney and Randy moved
to Ohio mid-school year

01:42:31.139 --> 01:42:32.849
to escape their own trauma in Woodsboro.

01:42:32.849 --> 01:42:36.760
While Scream 2 was set in Ohio, the college
that they filmed at was in Atlanta.

01:42:36.760 --> 01:42:39.510
Atlanta is mentioned several times in Scream
VI.

01:42:39.510 --> 01:42:42.139
It's the place where Jason and Greg first
met in middle school and

01:42:42.139 --> 01:42:44.639
Kirby works for the FBI's Atlanta field office.

01:42:44.639 --> 01:42:47.530
Could be a coincidence, or maybe an intentional
connection.

01:42:47.530 --> 01:42:51.260
Kirby, by the way, has a scar from where Charlie
stabbed her in Scre4m.

01:42:51.260 --> 01:42:53.929
She gets stabbed there again at the end of
Scream VI, but lives.

01:42:53.929 --> 01:42:56.010
The same way that Dewey survived in Scream
2.

01:42:56.010 --> 01:42:58.949
-[Paramedic] Knife went into most scar tissue.
Saved his life.

01:42:58.949 --> 01:43:01.100
The same thing seems to happen to Gale Weathers.

01:43:01.100 --> 01:43:04.070
In Scre4m, she got it in the shoulder and
that's where the new Ghostface

01:43:04.070 --> 01:43:06.990
gets her again in Six, and she manages to
survive.

01:43:06.990 --> 01:43:09.340
After Sam and Tara survive the first attack,
Gale Weathers comes

01:43:09.340 --> 01:43:12.239
investigating and the franchise tradition
of her getting punched in the face

01:43:12.239 --> 01:43:13.449
by the victim continues.

01:43:13.449 --> 01:43:16.449
If you look at the reaction of the cameraman,
it's the same look of surprise

01:43:16.449 --> 01:43:19.239
seen on the face of Joel, her camera guy in
Scream 2.

01:43:19.239 --> 01:43:20.949
– ♪ Yes I got that on film! ♪

01:43:20.949 --> 01:43:24.139
The reason Gale got punched was because Sam
was unhappy with her portrayal

01:43:24.139 --> 01:43:25.280
in Gale's latest Book.

01:43:25.280 --> 01:43:30.429
-[Sam] You called me unstable and a born killer.
-[Gale] That's taken out of context.

01:43:30.429 --> 01:43:31.940
-[Sam] That's literally a quote.

01:43:31.940 --> 01:43:35.550
which mirrors Dewey's frustrations with his
portrayal in the Woodsboro Murders book.

01:43:35.550 --> 01:43:37.670
He, too, supplies her with actual citations.

01:43:37.670 --> 01:43:40.530
-[Dewey] Page 41.

01:43:40.530 --> 01:43:43.570
As I mentioned, the first death out of the
main cast is Anika, and she's

01:43:43.570 --> 01:43:46.590
wearing the same green jacket that Randy wore
when he became a Ghostface

01:43:46.590 --> 01:43:48.090
victim in the second movie.

01:43:48.090 --> 01:43:50.699
There's also another reference to Randy when
they're walking through the park,

01:43:50.699 --> 01:43:52.300
trying to keep Ghostface on the line.

01:43:52.300 --> 01:43:54.199
And this similarity is called out by Mindy.

01:43:54.199 --> 01:43:56.909
-[Mindy] This is exactly how our Uncle Randy
died.

01:43:56.909 --> 01:44:00.300
However, the person in danger here is actually
Gale, who is at home.

01:44:00.300 --> 01:44:01.590
During her call with the killer,

01:44:01.590 --> 01:44:04.300
she tries to locate him by hanging up and
calling back.

01:44:04.300 --> 01:44:06.520
This is also a reference to Scream 2.

01:44:06.520 --> 01:44:09.860
-[Maureen] Hang the phone up as *69 his ***. Damn.

01:44:09.860 --> 01:44:11.340
The finale of each film takes

01:44:11.340 --> 01:44:13.409
place in a theater where all is revealed.

01:44:13.409 --> 01:44:16.320
At one point, Sam grabs a brick to defend
herself with, which honors the

01:44:16.320 --> 01:44:19.240
method by which Sidney tried to defeat Billy's
mother, crushing her with a

01:44:19.240 --> 01:44:20.719
pile of bricks.

01:44:20.719 --> 01:44:22.520
Quinn reveals herself as the final killer.

01:44:22.520 --> 01:44:26.130
-[Quinn] Hey my roomies. You didn’t see
that one coming, did you?

01:44:26.130 --> 01:44:30.840
Actually, I did. Billy and Roman already tried
faking their deaths, so it wasn't a huge surprise.

01:44:30.840 --> 01:44:33.640
More importantly, her line is almost identical
to what Mickey said

01:44:33.640 --> 01:44:35.600
after the reveal of Billy's mother.

01:44:35.600 --> 01:44:38.460
-[Roman] Nice twist, huh? Didn't see it coming,
did ya?

01:44:38.460 --> 01:44:41.699
Like in Scream 2, the main Ghostface the parent
of the antagonist from the

01:44:41.699 --> 01:44:44.300
previous movie, out for revenge against their
son's killer.

01:44:44.300 --> 01:44:47.540
The accomplice, or in this case, the accomplices
are enrolled at the

01:44:47.540 --> 01:44:50.900
university, their education being funded by
the other Ghostface.

01:44:50.900 --> 01:44:54.310
In Scream 2, Sidney is saved by Cotton, someone
she previously believed to

01:44:54.310 --> 01:44:55.310
be guilty.

01:44:55.310 --> 01:44:58.530
In Six, Sam and Tara are saved by Kirby, who
they thought was guilty.

01:44:58.530 --> 01:45:01.670
Finally, at the end, it turns out that Chad
is somehow alive, and he's

01:45:01.670 --> 01:45:04.219
carried out in a stretcher with Tara running
alongside.

01:45:04.219 --> 01:45:07.030
It's just like Dewey and Gale at the end of
Scream 2.

01:45:07.030 --> 01:45:10.020
But there would be even more Scream 2 references
in the movie theater,

01:45:10.020 --> 01:45:11.590
along with many more hidden secrets.

